{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09700","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09700","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malavika","familyName":"Suresh","name":"Suresh, Malavika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahaf","familyName":"Aljundi","name":"Aljundi, Rahaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ikechukwu","familyName":"Nkisi-Orji","name":"Nkisi-Orji, Ikechukwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalie","familyName":"Wiratunga","name":"Wiratunga, Nirmalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Layer Attention Probing for Fine-Grained Hallucination Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-04T14:37:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-16T13:07:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-19T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the large-scale adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) in various applications, there is a growing reliability concern due to their tendency to generate inaccurate text, i.e. hallucinations. In this work, we propose Cross-Layer Attention Probing (CLAP), a novel activation probing technique for hallucination detection, which processes the LLM activations across the entire residual stream as a joint sequence. Our empirical evaluations using five LLMs and three tasks show that CLAP improves hallucination detection compared to baselines on both greedy decoded responses as well as responses sampled at higher temperatures, thus enabling fine-grained detection, i.e. the ability to disambiguate hallucinations and non-hallucinations among different sampled responses to a given prompt. This allows us to propose a detect-then-mitigate strategy using CLAP to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM reliability compared to direct mitigation approaches. Finally, we show that CLAP maintains high reliability even when applied out-of-distribution. Code available at: https://github.com/itsmemala/CLAP"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the TRUST-AI workshop, ECAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09700","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-19T02:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiří","familyName":"Milička","name":"Milička, Jiří","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Marklová","name":"Marklová, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Václav","familyName":"Cvrček","name":"Cvrček, Václav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmark of stylistic variation in LLM-generated texts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:12:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:32:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T23:31:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-21T00:15:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1075/ijcl.25151.mil","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates the register variation in texts written by humans and comparable texts produced by large language models (LLMs). Biber's multidimensional analysis (MDA) is applied to a sample of human-written texts and AI-created texts generated to be their counterparts to find the dimensions of variation in which LLMs differ most significantly and most systematically from humans. As textual material, a new LLM-generated corpus AI-Brown is used, which is comparable to BE-21 (a Brown family corpus representing contemporary British English). Since all languages except English are underrepresented in the training data of frontier LLMs, similar analysis is replicated on Czech using AI-Koditex corpus and Czech multidimensional model. Examined were 16 frontier models in various settings and prompts, with emphasis placed on the difference between base models and instruction-tuned models. Based on this, a benchmark is created through which models can be compared with each other and ranked in interpretable dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Data and scripts: https://osf.io/hs7xt/. Interactive charts: https://www.korpus.cz/stylisticbenchmark/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-21T02:23:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sepehr Golrokh","familyName":"Amin","name":"Amin, Sepehr Golrokh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Devin","familyName":"Rhoads","name":"Rhoads, Devin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Fakhrmoosavi","name":"Fakhrmoosavi, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas E.","familyName":"Lownes","name":"Lownes, Nicholas E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John N.","familyName":"Ivan","name":"Ivan, John N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generating Individual Travel Diaries Using Large Language Models Informed by Census and Land-Use Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T17:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T23:59:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:18:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T15:21:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-16T01:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study introduces a Large Language Model (LLM) scheme for generating key attributes of travel diaries in agent-based transportation models, including purpose, mode and distance, to assess the underlying viability of LLMs for activity generation tasks. While traditional approaches rely on large quantities of proprietary household travel surveys, our method generates personas stochastically from open-source American Community Survey (ACS) and Smart Location Database (SLD) data, then synthesizes diaries through direct prompting. Our study features a novel one-to-cohort realism score: a composite of four metrics (Trip Count Score, Interval Score, Purpose Score, and Mode Score) validated against the Connecticut Statewide Transportation Study (CSTS) diaries, matched across demographic variables. Our validation utilizes Jensen-Shannon Divergence to measure distributional similarities between generated and real diaries. When compared to diaries generated with classical methods (Negative Binomial for trip generation; Multinomial Logit for mode/purpose) calibrated on the validation set, LLM generated diaries achieve comparable overall realism (LLM mean: 0.692 vs. 0.628). The LLM excels in determining trip purpose, and its trip mode predictions demonstrate greater consistency (a narrower Realism Score distribution). Meanwhile, classical models lead to better numerical estimates of trip count and activity duration. Aggregate validation confirms the LLM's statistical representativeness (LLM mean: 0.779 vs. 0.706), demonstrating LLM's zero-shot viability and establishing a quantifiable metric of diary realism for future synthetic diary evaluation systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-16T02:40:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshat","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Akshat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karun","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Karun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WhisTLE: Deeply Supervised, Text-Only Domain Adaptation for Small Pretrained Speech Recognition Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:59:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-25T17:57:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T02:04:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T15:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-09T01:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained automatic speech recognition (ASR) models such as Whisper perform well but still need domain adaptation to handle unseen parlance. In many real-world settings, collecting speech data is impractical, necessitating text-only adaptation. We propose WhisTLE, a deeply supervised, text-only adaptation method for pretrained encoder-decoder ASR models. WhisTLE trains a variational autoencoder (VAE) to model encoder outputs from text and fine-tunes the decoder using the learned text-to-latent encoder, optionally combined with text-to-speech (TTS) adaptation. At inference, the original encoder is restored, incurring no extra runtime cost. Across four datasets and four ASR models, WhisTLE alone helps in 28 of 32 (88%) cases, with an average relative WER drop of 22%. Using WhisTLE alone or in combination with other adaptation approaches helps in 116 of 144 (81%) cases, with an average additional WER drop of 15%. Given all treatments that improve on baseline scores, WhisTLE helps in 45 of 55 (82%) cases, with an average additional relative WER drop of 16%. WhisTLE with TTS reduces word error rate (WER) by a relative 49% and outperforms all non-WhisTLE baselines in 100 of 112 scenarios. We also find that WhisTLE additively complements any combination of other domain adaptation approaches; we thus recommend the inclusion of WhisTLE during standard processes for adapting encoder-decoder ASR models. Our code is at https://github.com/akshat0123/WhisTLE"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP 2026 main conference, 11 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:20Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:53:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Dongmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonah","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yoonah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongwook","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jongwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eun-Ju","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Eun-Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yohan","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human Psychometric Questionnaires Mischaracterize LLM Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:14:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:25:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-18T10:00:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:02:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-03T11:04:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-29T15:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-01T01:09:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-09-02T18:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-04T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We examine whether human psychometric questionnaires can serve as reliable tools for characterizing and predicting LLM behavior in everyday user interactions. We analyze eight open-source LLMs by comparing their value and personality profiles derived from two different methods: Likert self-reports on established questionnaires (PVQ-40/21 and BFI-44/10) and generation probabilities over value-laden responses to everyday user queries. The two profiles diverge substantially. Within-construct item consistency, often cited as evidence of stable LLM dispositions, disappears in generation probabilities. We find that established questionnaire items contain explicit lexical cues that allow models to recognize the target construct and respond in alignment-consistent, socially desirable ways, whereas realistic user queries contain far less recognizable cues. In addition, demographic persona prompts shift models' responses to human questionnaires in ways consistent with real human patterns, but no such shifts appear in the generation setting, highlighting that human questionnaires overestimate LLMs' ability to faithfully reproduce expected psychological traits when role-playing demographic personas. Overall, our study indicates that questionnaire scores alone should not be treated as evidence of LLMs' response tendencies in realistic user interactions, and supports generation-probability profiling with ecologically valid items as a complementary behavioral measure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:34Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-04T02:37:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elizaveta D.","familyName":"Moskovskaya","name":"Moskovskaya, Elizaveta D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton D.","familyName":"Moscowsky","name":"Moscowsky, Anton D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robot guide with multi-agent control and automatic scenario generation with LLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; I.2.7; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"93C85","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T14:59:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-07T10:11:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-10T00:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The article describes the development of a hybrid social robot control architecture to overcome the limitations of traditional approaches, where behavior scripts manually synchronize the robot's actions and text, and existing methods focus primarily on short dialogue responses. The architecture of the proposed system combines a multi-agent resource management system with automatic generation of behavior scenarios based on large language models. This system automates the preparation of text and commands for the robot's non-verbal behavior for extended narratives and resolves resource conflicts between multiple execution mechanisms. The system was tested on the MENTOR-1 tour guide robot, for which it successfully generated scenarios automatically and demonstrated more natural and rich behavior compared to existing approaches. The proposed approach provides full automation of both scenario preparation and execution through efficient resource management, enhancing the quality of social robot interaction in long-term storytelling tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 4 figures, 4 tables, 1 demo-video and repository link. There were major changes: an introduction, a review, and a new experiment. Some tables and figures have also been changed"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:10Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-10T02:05:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10406","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10406","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10406","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupert","familyName":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, Rupert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Kersting","name":"Kersting, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multipole Semantic Attention: A Fast Approximation of Softmax Attention for Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68W25, 68T50 (primary) 68W40, 68T07 (secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T15:24:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:31:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-13T12:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:40:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T13:36:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-01T00:59:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretraining transformers on long sequences (entire code repositories, collections of related documents) is bottlenecked by quadratic attention costs. We present Multipole Semantic Attention (MuSe), which accelerates 64k-context pretraining by 36% while matching baseline loss, requiring no architectural changes. MuSe clusters queries and keys separately in representation space. This yields query-specific summaries that substantially outperform spatial blocking at matched sparsity, while also enabling drop-in compatibility with existing pretrained models; we validate on Llama 3.1-8B and 3.2-1B without retraining. We pretrain language models up to 1B parameters at 64k context on code and scientific documents, confirming that MuSe preserves quality and long-context utilization during training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10406","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:14Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-01T02:27:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Keyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongsheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyue","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Ziyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yicun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeping","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Hongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangnan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Guangnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfeng","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Hongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limited Reference, Reliable Generation: A Two-Component Framework for Tabular Data Generation in Low-Data Regimes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T04:34:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:15:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-25T13:10:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic tabular data generation is increasingly essential in machine learning, supporting downstream applications when real-world, high-quality tabular data is insufficient. Existing tabular generation approaches, such as generative adversarial networks (GANs) and fine-tuned Large Language Models (LLMs), typically require sufficient reference data, limiting their effectiveness in domain-specific datasets with scarce records. While prompt-based LLMs offer flexibility without parameter tuning, they often generate distributionally drifted data with localized redundancy, leading to degradation in downstream task performance. To overcome these issues, we propose ReFine, a framework that (i) extracts symbolic if-then rules from interpretable models and embeds them into prompts to explicitly guide the generation process toward the domain-specific distribution, and (ii) applies dual-granularity filtering that mitigates over-sampling patterns while preserving rare but informative samples to reduce localized redundancy. Extensive experiments on diverse benchmarks demonstrate that ReFine provides robust downstream utility, achieving a top-tier average rank across datasets and data regimes, with an average relative improvement of 7.48% in extreme low-data regimes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:42Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-26T02:16:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Sassoon","name":"Sassoon, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Szczepanski","name":"Szczepanski, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martyna","familyName":"Poreba","name":"Poreba, Martyna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"I-Segmenter: Integer-Only Vision Transformer for Efficient Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:14:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:41:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T10:40:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:38:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have recently achieved strong results in semantic segmentation, yet their deployment on resource-constrained devices remains limited due to their high memory footprint and computational cost. Quantization offers an effective strategy to improve efficiency, but ViT-based segmentation models are notoriously fragile under low precision, as quantization errors accumulate across deep encoder-decoder pipelines. We introduce I-Segmenter, the first fully integer-only ViT segmentation framework. Building on the Segmenter architecture, I-Segmenter systematically replaces floating-point operations with integer-only counterparts. To further stabilize both training and inference, we propose $λ$-ShiftGELU, a novel activation function that mitigates the limitations of uniform quantization in handling long-tailed activation distributions. In addition, we remove the L2 normalization layer and replace bilinear interpolation in the decoder with nearest neighbor upsampling, ensuring integer-only execution throughout the computational graph. Extensive experiments show that I-Segmenter achieves accuracy within a reasonable margin of its FP32 baseline (5.1 % on average), while reducing model size by up to 3.8x and enabling up to 1.2x faster inference with optimized runtimes. Notably, even in one-shot PTQ with a single calibration image, I-Segmenter delivers competitive accuracy, underscoring its practicality for real-world deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by the Journal of Systems Architecture"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:33Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:12:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai R.","familyName":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Kai R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roland M.","familyName":"Mueller","name":"Mueller, Roland M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikko","familyName":"Rönkkö","name":"Rönkkö, Mikko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Starzl","name":"Starzl, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"Edmondson","name":"Edmondson, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALIGNS: Unlocking nomological networks in psychological measurement through a large language model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; J.4; I.5.1; H.3.3; H.2.8","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T04:21:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T16:46:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:50:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-02T11:26:30Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; Error in algorithm explanation"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:30:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Psychological measurement is critical to many disciplines. Despite advances in measurement, building nomological networks, theoretical maps of how concepts and measures relate to establish validity, remains a challenge 70 years after Cronbach and Meehl proposed them as fundamental to validation. This limitation has practical consequences: clinical trials may fail to detect treatment effects, and public policy may target the wrong outcomes. We introduce Analysis of Latent Indicators to Generate Nomological Structures (ALIGNS), a large language model-based system trained with validated questionnaire measures. ALIGNS provides three comprehensive nomological networks containing over 550,000 indicators across psychology, medicine, social policy, and other fields. This represents the first application of large language models to solve a foundational problem in measurement validation. We report classification accuracy tests used to develop the model, as well as three evaluations. In the first evaluation, the widely used NIH PROMIS anxiety and depression instruments are shown to converge into a single dimension of emotional distress. The second evaluation examines child temperament measures and identifies four potential dimensions not captured by current frameworks, and questions one existing dimension. The third evaluation, an applicability check, engages expert psychometricians who assess the system's importance, accessibility, and suitability. ALIGNS is freely available at nomologicalnetwork.org, complementing traditional validation methods with large-scale nomological analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Error in algorithm explanation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:07Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hasibur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Hasibur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smit","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Smit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents' Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:08:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T01:28:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:15:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3790388","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) enable conversational agents (CAs) to express distinctive personalities, raising new questions about how such designs shape user perceptions. This study investigates how personality expression levels and user-agent personality alignment influence perceptions in goal-oriented tasks. In a between-subjects experiment (N=150), participants completed travel planning with CAs exhibiting low, medium, or high expression across the Big Five traits, controlled via our novel Trait Modulation Keys framework. Results revealed an inverted-U relationship: medium expression produced the most positive evaluations across Intelligence, Enjoyment, Anthropomorphism, Intention to Adopt, Trust, and Likeability, significantly outperforming both extremes. Personality alignment further enhanced outcomes, with Extraversion and Emotional Stability emerging as the most influential traits. Cluster analysis identified three distinct compatibility profiles, with \"Well-Aligned\" users reporting substantially positive perceptions. These findings demonstrate that personality expression and strategic trait alignment constitute optimal design targets for CA personality, offering design implications as LLM-based CAs become increasingly prevalent."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, CHI 2026 conference paper (article no. 371)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:34Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09708","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09708","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09708","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nirmalendu","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Nirmalendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeo Wei","familyName":"Jie","name":"Jie, Yeo Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Abdullah","name":"Abdullah, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjan","familyName":"Satapathy","name":"Satapathy, Ranjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erik","familyName":"Cambria","name":"Cambria, Erik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka Wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond I'm Sorry, I Can't: Dissecting Large Language Model Refusal"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T02:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T07:46:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:31:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T03:29:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:21:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Refusal on harmful prompts is a key safety behaviour in instruction-tuned large language models (LLMs), yet the internal causes of this behaviour remain poorly understood. We study two public instruction-tuned models, Gemma-2-2B-IT and LLaMA-3.1-8B-IT, using sparse autoencoders (SAEs) trained on residual-stream activations. Given a harmful prompt, we search the SAE latent space for feature sets whose ablation flips the model from refusal to compliance, demonstrating causal influence and creating a jailbreak. Our search proceeds in three stages: (1) Refusal Direction: find a refusal-mediating direction and collect SAE features near that direction; (2) Greedy Filtering: prune to a minimal set; and (3) Interaction Discovery: fit a factorization machine (FM) that captures nonlinear interactions among the remaining active features and the minimal set. This pipeline yields a broad set of jailbreak-critical features, offering insight into the mechanistic basis of refusal. Moreover, we find evidence of redundant features that remain dormant unless earlier features are suppressed. Our findings highlight the potential for fine-grained auditing and targeted intervention in safety behaviours by manipulating the interpretable latent space."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09708","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-29T02:31:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saarth","familyName":"Gaonkar","name":"Gaonkar, Saarth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishabh","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Rishabh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kurt","familyName":"Keutzer","name":"Keutzer, Kurt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitriy","familyName":"Morozov","name":"Morozov, Dmitriy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Mahoney","name":"Mahoney, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Gholami","name":"Gholami, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SciML Agents: Write the Solver, Not the Solution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T02:53:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:07:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work in scientific machine learning aims to tackle scientific tasks directly by predicting target values with neural networks (e.g., physics-informed neural networks, neural ODEs, neural operators, etc.), but attaining high accuracy and robustness has been challenging. We explore an alternative view: use LLMs to write code that leverages decades of numerical algorithms. This shifts the burden from learning a solution function to making domain-aware numerical choices. We ask whether LLMs can act as SciML agents that, given a natural-language ODE description, generate runnable code that is scientifically appropriate, selecting suitable solvers (stiff vs. non-stiff), and enforcing stability checks. There is currently no benchmark to measure this kind of capability for scientific computing tasks. As such, we first introduce two new datasets: a diagnostic dataset of adversarial \"misleading\" problems; and a large-scale benchmark of 1,000 diverse ODE tasks. The diagnostic set contains problems whose superficial appearance suggests stiffness, and that require algebraic simplification to demonstrate non-stiffness; and the large-scale benchmark spans stiff and non-stiff ODE regimes. We evaluate open- and closed-source LLM models along two axes: (i) unguided versus guided prompting with domain-specific knowledge; and (ii) off-the-shelf versus fine-tuned variants. Our evaluation measures both executability and numerical validity against reference solutions. We find that with sufficient context and guided prompts, newer instruction-following models achieve high accuracy on both criteria. In many cases, recent open-source systems perform strongly without fine-tuning, while older or smaller models still benefit from fine-tuning. Overall, our preliminary results indicate that careful prompting and fine-tuning can yield a specialized LLM agent capable of reliably solving simple ODE problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:09Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-15T02:23:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09926","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09926","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09926","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LoFT: Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning for Long-tailed Semi-Supervised Learning in Open-World Scenarios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T02:28:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:12:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T15:36:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:34:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T15:31:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:54:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T11:46:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:24:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-08T02:30:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-09T00:19:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-tailed semi-supervised learning (LTSSL) presents a formidable challenge where models must overcome the scarcity of tail samples while mitigating the noise from unreliable pseudo-labels. Most prior LTSSL methods are designed to train models from scratch, which often leads to issues such as overconfidence and low-quality pseudo-labels. To address this problem, we first theoretically prove that utilizing a foundation model significantly reduces the hypothesis complexity, which tightens the generalization bound and in turn minimizes the Balanced Posterior Error (BPE). Furthermore, we demonstrate that the feature compactness of foundation models strictly compresses the acceptance region for outliers, providing a geometric guarantee for robustness. Motivated by these theoretical insights, we extend LTSSL into the foundation model fine-tuning paradigm and propose a novel framework: LoFT (Long-tailed semi-supervised learning via parameter-efficient Fine-Tuning). Furthermore, we explore a more practical setting by investigating semi-supervised learning under open-world conditions, where the unlabeled data may include out-of-distribution (OOD) samples.To handle this problem, we propose LoFT-OW (LoFT under Open-World scenarios) to improve the discriminative ability. Experimental results on multiple benchmarks demonstrate that our method achieves superior performance. Code is available: https://github.com/games-liker/LoFT"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09926","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:22:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:22:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-09T02:18:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tilen","familyName":"Cadez","name":"Cadez, Tilen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyoung-Min","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kyoung-Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural Scaling Laws for Deep Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Other Condensed Matter (cond-mat.other)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T06:49:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:19:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T13:26:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:15:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/2632-2153/ae484c","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural scaling laws--power-law relationships between generalization errors and characteristics of deep learning models--are vital tools for developing reliable models while managing limited resources. Although the success of large language models highlights the importance of these laws, their application to deep regression models remains largely unexplored. Here, we empirically investigate neural scaling laws in deep regression using a parameter estimation model for twisted van der Waals magnets. We observe power-law relationships between the loss and both training dataset size and model capacity across a wide range of values, employing various architectures--including fully connected networks, residual networks, and vision transformers. Furthermore, the scaling exponents governing these relationships range from 1 to 2, with specific values depending on the regressed parameters and model details. The consistent scaling behaviors and their large scaling exponents suggest that the performance of deep regression models can improve substantially with increasing data size."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Supplementary Information will be provided with the published manuscript"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:24:40Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:21:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leen","familyName":"Almajed","name":"Almajed, Leen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abeer","familyName":"ALdayel","name":"ALdayel, Abeer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incongruent Positivity: When Miscalibrated Positivity Undermines Online Supportive Conversations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:25:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:33:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T18:44:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-19T00:08:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In emotionally supportive conversations, well-intended positivity can sometimes misfire, leading to responses that feel dismissive, minimizing, or unrealistically optimistic. We examine this phenomenon of incongruent positivity as miscalibrated expressions of positive support in both human and LLM generated responses. To this end, we collected real user-assistant dialogues from Reddit across a range of emotional intensities and generated additional responses using large language models for the same context. We categorize these conversations by intensity into two levels: Mild, which covers relationship tension and general advice, and Severe, which covers grief and anxiety conversations. This level of categorization enables a comparative analysis of how supportive responses vary across lower and higher stakes contexts. Our analysis reveals that LLMs are more prone to unrealistic positivity through dismissive and minimizing tone, particularly in high-stakes contexts. To further study the underlying dimensions of this phenomenon, we finetune LLMs on datasets with strong and weak emotional reactions. Moreover, we developed a weakly supervised multilabel classifier ensemble (DeBERTa and MentalBERT) that shows improved detection of incongruent positivity types across two sorts of concerns (Mild and Severe). Our findings shed light on the need to move beyond merely generating generic positive responses and instead study the congruent support measures to balance positive affect with emotional acknowledgment. This approach offers insights into aligning large language models with affective expectations in the online supportive dialogue, paving the way toward context-aware and trust preserving online conversation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in ICWSM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:03Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:53:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junqiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianze","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Tianze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xianzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Chuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Meta-Learning Reinforcement Learning for Crypto-Return Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T14:20:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-01T10:15:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T02:06:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting cryptocurrency returns is notoriously difficult: price movements are driven by a fast-shifting blend of on-chain activity, news flow, and social sentiment, while labeled training data are scarce and expensive. In this paper, we present Meta-RL-Crypto, a unified transformer-based architecture that unifies meta-learning and reinforcement learning (RL) to create a fully self-improving trading agent. Starting from a vanilla instruction-tuned LLM, the agent iteratively alternates between three roles-actor, judge, and meta-judge-in a closed-loop architecture. This learning process requires no additional human supervision. It can leverage multimodal market inputs and internal preference feedback. The agent in the system continuously refines both the trading policy and evaluation criteria. Experiments across diverse market regimes demonstrate that Meta-RL-Crypto shows good performance on the technical indicators of the real market and outperforming other LLM-based baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:38:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yikuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiazun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirui","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Yirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suifeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Suifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weipeng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Weipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaorui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DB3 Team's Solution For Meta KDD Cup' 25"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-12T08:27:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T05:54:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:05:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the db3 team's winning solution for the Meta CRAG-MM Challenge 2025 at KDD Cup'25. Addressing the challenge's unique multi-modal, multi-turn question answering benchmark (CRAG-MM), we developed a comprehensive framework that integrates tailored retrieval pipelines for different tasks with a unified LLM-tuning approach for hallucination control. Our solution features (1) domain-specific retrieval pipelines handling image-indexed knowledge graphs, web sources, and multi-turn conversations; and (2) advanced refusal training using SFT, DPO, and RL. The system achieved 2nd place in Task 1, 2nd place in Task 2, and 1st place in Task 3, securing the grand prize for excellence in ego-centric queries through superior handling of first-person perspective challenges."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:09Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:25:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10436","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10436","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10436","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shadikur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Shadikur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aroosa","familyName":"Hameed","name":"Hameed, Aroosa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautam","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Gautam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed Muhammad","familyName":"Danish","name":"Danish, Syed Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RefactorCoderQA: Benchmarking LLMs for Multi-Domain Coding Question Solutions in Cloud and Edge Deployment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:44:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-25T19:44:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-29T01:27:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To optimize the reasoning and problem-solving capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs), we propose a novel cloud-edge collaborative architecture that enables a structured multi-agent prompting framework. This framework comprises three specialized components: GuideLLM, a lightweight model deployed at the edge to provide methodological guidance; SolverLLM, a more powerful model hosted in the cloud and responsible for generating code solutions; and JudgeLLM, an automated evaluator for assessing solution correctness and quality. To evaluate and demonstrate the effectiveness of this architecture in realistic settings, we introduce RefactorCoderQA, a comprehensive benchmark designed to evaluate and enhance the performance of LLMs across multi-domain coding tasks. Motivated by the limitations of existing benchmarks, RefactorCoderQA systematically covers multiple technical domains, including Software Engineering, Data Science, Machine Learning, and Natural Language Processing, using authentic coding challenges sourced from Stack Overflow. We propose RefactorCoder-MoE, a fine-tuned mixture-of-experts (MoE) code language model based on DeepSeek-Coder-7B-Instruct, adapted to the RefactorCoderQA benchmark using QLoRA for domain-specific coding question answering. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RefactorCoder-MoE achieves strong and competitive performance, significantly outperforming all evaluated open-source and commercial baselines, with an overall accuracy of 76.84%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures, Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Services Computing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10436","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:57Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-29T02:42:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Khaled","name":"Khaled, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyen","familyName":"Kale","name":"Kale, Satyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Douillard","name":"Douillard, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rob","familyName":"Fergus","name":"Fergus, Rob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manzil","familyName":"Zaheer","name":"Zaheer, Manzil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Understanding Outer Optimizers in Local SGD: Learning Rates, Momentum, and Acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:47:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-11T17:09:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:58:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern machine learning often requires training with large batch size, distributed data, and massively parallel compute hardware (like mobile and other edge devices or distributed data centers). Communication becomes a major bottleneck in such settings but methods like Local Stochastic Gradient Descent (Local SGD) show great promise in reducing this additional communication overhead. Local SGD consists of three parts: a local optimization process, an aggregation mechanism, and an outer optimizer that uses the aggregated updates from the nodes to produce a new model. While there exists an extensive literature on understanding the impact of hyperparameters in the local optimization process, the choice of outer optimizer and its hyperparameters is less clear. We study the role of the outer optimizer in Local SGD, and prove new convergence guarantees for the algorithm. In particular, we show that tuning the outer learning rate allows us to (a) trade off between optimization error and stochastic gradient noise variance, and (b) make up for ill-tuning of the inner learning rate. Our theory suggests that the outer learning rate should sometimes be set to values greater than $1$. We extend our results to settings where we use momentum in the outer optimizer, and we show a similar role for the momentum-adjusted outer learning rate. We also study acceleration in the outer optimizer and show that it improves the convergence rate as a function of the number of communication rounds, improving upon the convergence rate of prior algorithms that apply acceleration locally. Finally, we also introduce a novel data-dependent analysis of Local SGD that yields further insights on outer learning rate tuning. We conduct comprehensive experiments with standard language models and various outer optimizers to validate our theory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:02Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09711","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09711","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09711","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aya E.","familyName":"Fouda","name":"Fouda, Aya E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrahamn A.","familyName":"Hassan","name":"Hassan, Abdelrahamn A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radwa J.","familyName":"Hanafy","name":"Hanafy, Radwa J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed E.","familyName":"Fouda","name":"Fouda, Mohammed E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PsychiatryBench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T20:57:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-23T15:40:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) offer significant potential in enhancing psychiatric practice, from improving diagnostic accuracy to streamlining clinical documentation and therapeutic support. However, existing evaluation resources heavily rely on small clinical interview corpora, social media posts, or synthetic dialogues, which limits their clinical validity and fails to capture the full complexity of diagnostic reasoning. In this work, we introduce PsychiatryBench, a rigorously curated benchmark grounded exclusively in authoritative, expert-validated psychiatric textbooks and casebooks. PsychiatryBench comprises eleven distinct question-answering tasks ranging from diagnostic reasoning and treatment planning to longitudinal follow-up, management planning, clinical approach, sequential case analysis, and multiple-choice/extended matching formats totaling 5,188 expert-annotated items. {\\color{red}We evaluate a diverse set of frontier LLMs (including Google Gemini, DeepSeek, Sonnet 4.5, and GPT 5) alongside leading open-source medical models such as MedGemma using both conventional metrics and an \"LLM-as-judge\" similarity scoring framework. Our results reveal substantial gaps in clinical consistency and safety, particularly in multi-turn follow-up and management tasks, underscoring the need for specialized model tuning and more robust evaluation paradigms. PsychiatryBench offers a modular, extensible platform for benchmarking and improving LLM performance in mental health applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09711","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:50Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:49:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninad","familyName":"Bhat","name":"Bhat, Ninad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kieran","familyName":"Browne","name":"Browne, Kieran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pip","familyName":"Bingemann","name":"Bingemann, Pip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Creativity Benchmark: A benchmark for marketing creativity for large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T04:44:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T23:04:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T01:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Creativity Benchmark, an evaluation framework for large language models (LLMs) in marketing creativity. The benchmark covers 100 brands (12 categories) and three prompt types (Insights, Ideas, Wild Ideas). Human pairwise preferences from 678 practising creatives over 11,012 anonymised comparisons, analysed with Bradley-Terry models, show tightly clustered performance with no model dominating across brands or prompt types: the top-bottom spread is $Δθ\\approx 0.45$, which implies a head-to-head win probability of $0.61$; the highest-rated model beats the lowest only about $61\\%$ of the time. We also analyse model diversity using cosine distances to capture intra- and inter-model variation and sensitivity to prompt reframing. Comparing three LLM-as-judge setups with human rankings reveals weak, inconsistent correlations and judge-specific biases, underscoring that automated judges cannot substitute for human evaluation. Conventional creativity tests also transfer only partially to brand-constrained tasks. Overall, the results highlight the need for expert human evaluation and diversity-aware workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 Pages, 14 figures. Fixed typos"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:38Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:40:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepDive: Advancing Deep Search Agents with Knowledge Graphs and Multi-Turn RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:52:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:46:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T13:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:51:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Augmenting large language models (LLMs) with browsing tools substantially improves their potential as deep search agents to solve complex, real-world tasks. Yet, open LLMs still perform poorly in such settings due to limited long-horizon reasoning capacity with browsing tools and the lack of sufficiently difficult supervised data. To address these challenges, we present DeepDive to advance deep search agents. First, we propose a strategy to automatically synthesize complex, difficult, and hard-to-find questions from open knowledge graphs. Second, we apply end-to-end multi-turn reinforcement learning (RL) to enhance LLMs' long-horizon reasoning with deep search. To encourage diversity and reduce redundancy, we design a redundancy penalty that discourages repeated similar queries. Experiments show that DeepDive-32B achieves a new open-source competitive result on BrowseComp, outperforming WebSailor, DeepSeek-R1-Browse, and Search-o1. We demonstrate that multi-turn RL training improves deep search ability and significantly contributes to the performance improvements across multiple benchmarks. We observe that DeepDive enables test-time scaling of tool calls and parallel sampling. All datasets, models, and code are publicly available at https://github.com/THUDM/DeepDive."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:35:12Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:35:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:21:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Holzschuh","name":"Holzschuh, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Kohl","name":"Kohl, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Redinger","name":"Redinger, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Thuerey","name":"Thuerey, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P3D: Scalable Neural Surrogates for High-Resolution 3D Physics Simulations with Global Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:26:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:33:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T10:19:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:39:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a scalable framework for learning deterministic and probabilistic neural surrogates for high-resolution 3D physics simulations. We introduce a hybrid CNN-Transformer backbone architecture targeted for 3D physics simulations, which significantly outperforms existing architectures in terms of speed and accuracy. Our proposed network can be pretrained on small patches of the simulation domain, which can be fused to obtain a global solution, optionally guided via a fast and scalable sequence-to-sequence model to include long-range dependencies. This setup allows for training large-scale models with reduced memory and compute requirements for high-resolution datasets. We evaluate our backbone architecture against a large set of baseline methods with the objective to simultaneously learn the dynamics of 14 different types of PDEs in 3D. We demonstrate how to scale our model to high-resolution isotropic turbulence with spatial resolutions of up to $512^3$. Finally, we demonstrate the versatility of our network by training it as a diffusion model to produce probabilistic samples of highly turbulent 3D channel flows across varying Reynolds numbers, accurately capturing the underlying flow statistics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:06Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:20:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxun","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Jianxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianfu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tianfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaize","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kaize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas Jing","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Nicholas Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Population-Aligned Persona Generation for LLM-based Social Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T10:43:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:29:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-04T09:04:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled human-like social simulations at unprecedented scale and fidelity, offering new opportunities for computational social science. A key challenge, however, is the construction of persona sets that authentically represent the diversity and distribution of real-world populations. Most existing LLM-based social simulation studies focus primarily on designing agentic frameworks and simulation environments, often overlooking the complexities of persona generation and the potential biases introduced by unrepresentative persona sets. In this paper, we propose a systematic framework for synthesizing high-quality, population-aligned persona sets for LLM-driven social simulation. Our approach begins by leveraging LLMs to generate narrative personas from long-term social media data, followed by rigorous quality assessment to filter out low-fidelity profiles. We then apply importance sampling to achieve global alignment with reference psychometric distributions, such as the Big Five personality traits. To address the needs of specific simulation contexts, we further introduce a task-specific module that adapts the globally aligned persona set to targeted subpopulations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method significantly reduces population-level bias and enables accurate, flexible social simulation for a wide range of research and policy applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:46:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miklós","familyName":"Sebők","name":"Sebők, Miklós","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viktor","familyName":"Kovács","name":"Kovács, Viktor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Bánóczy","name":"Bánóczy, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel Møller","familyName":"Eriksen","name":"Eriksen, Daniel Møller","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathalie","familyName":"Neptune","name":"Neptune, Nathalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Roussille","name":"Roussille, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Token Limits: Assessing Language Model Performance on Long Text Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.7; I.2; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:47:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:33:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T22:00:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:10:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T23:22:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:15:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The most widely used large language models in the social sciences (such as BERT, and its derivatives, e.g. RoBERTa) have a limitation on the input text length that they can process to produce predictions. This is a particularly pressing issue for some classification tasks, where the aim is to handle long input texts. One such area deals with laws and draft laws (bills), which can have a length of multiple hundred pages and, therefore, are not particularly amenable for processing with models that can only handle e.g. 512 tokens. In this paper, we show results from experiments covering 5 languages with XLM-RoBERTa, Longformer, GPT-3.5, GPT-4 models for the multiclass classification task of the Comparative Agendas Project, which has a codebook of 21 policy topic labels from education to health care. Results show no particular advantage for the Longformer model, pre-trained specifically for the purposes of handling long inputs. The comparison between the GPT variants and the best-performing open model yielded an edge for the latter. An analysis of class-level factors points to the importance of support and substance overlaps between specific categories when it comes to performance on long text inputs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:24Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:10:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10248","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10248","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10248","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janis","familyName":"Keuper","name":"Keuper, Janis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Injection Attacks on LLM Generated Reviews of Scientific Publications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T13:45:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:36:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T04:07:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T12:26:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:45:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ongoing intense discussion on rising LLM usage in the scientific peer-review process has recently been mingled by reports of authors using hidden prompt injections to manipulate review scores. Since the existence of such \"attacks\" - although seen by some commentators as \"self-defense\" - would have a great impact on the further debate, this paper investigates the practicability and technical success of the described manipulations. Our systematic evaluation uses 1k reviews of 2024 ICLR papers generated by a wide range of LLMs shows two distinct results: I) very simple prompt injections are indeed highly effective, reaching up to 100% acceptance scores. II) LLM reviews are generally biased toward acceptance (\u0026gt;95% in many models). Both results have great impact on the ongoing discussions on LLM usage in peer-review."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10248","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:30:32Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:30:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-26T02:15:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alva","familyName":"West","name":"West, Alva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Minjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Ning","name":"Ning, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Abduct, Act, Predict: Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:51:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T14:45:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Failure attribution in multi-agent systems -- pinpointing the exact step where a decisive error occurs -- is a critical yet unsolved challenge. Current methods treat this as a pattern recognition task over long conversation logs, leading to critically low step-level accuracy (below 17\\%), which renders them impractical for debugging complex systems. Their core weakness is a fundamental inability to perform robust counterfactual reasoning: to determine if correcting a single action would have actually averted the task failure. To bridge this \\emph{counterfactual inference gap}, we introduce Abduct-Act-Predict (A2P) Scaffolding, a novel agent framework that transforms failure attribution from pattern recognition into a structured causal inference task. A2P explicitly guides a large language model through a formal three-step reasoning process within a single inference pass: (1) Abduction, to infer the hidden root causes behind an agent's actions; (2) Action, to define a minimal corrective intervention; and (3) Prediction, to simulate the subsequent trajectory and verify if the intervention resolves the failure. This structured approach leverages the holistic context of the entire conversation while imposing a rigorous causal logic on the model's analysis. Our extensive experiments on the Who\\\u0026amp;When benchmark demonstrate its efficacy. On the Algorithm-Generated dataset, A2P achieves 47.46\\% step-level accuracy, a 2.85$\\times$ improvement over the 16.67\\% of the baseline. On the more complex Hand-Crafted dataset, it achieves 29.31\\% step accuracy, a 2.43$\\times$ improvement over the baseline's 12.07\\%. By reframing the problem through a causal lens, A2P Scaffolding provides a robust, verifiable, and significantly more accurate solution for automated failure attribution. Ours code are released at https://github.com/ResearAI/A2P."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09716","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09716","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09716","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kexin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DongXiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, DongXiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengtao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Tengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Qinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VStyle: A Benchmark for Voice Style Adaptation with Spoken Instructions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T14:28:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T02:40:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:09:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Spoken language models (SLMs) have emerged as a unified paradigm for speech understanding and generation, enabling natural human machine interaction. However, while most progress has focused on semantic accuracy and instruction following, the ability of SLMs to adapt their speaking style based on spoken instructions has received limited attention. We introduce Voice Style Adaptation (VSA), a new task that examines whether SLMs can modify their speaking style, such as timbre, prosody, or persona following natural language spoken commands. To study this task, we present VStyle, a bilingual (Chinese \u0026amp; English) benchmark covering four categories of speech generation: acoustic attributes, natural language instruction, role play, and implicit empathy. We also introduce the Large Audio Language Model as a Judge (LALM as a Judge) framework, which progressively evaluates outputs along textual faithfulness, style adherence, and naturalness, ensuring reproducible and objective assessment. Experiments on commercial systems and open source SLMs demonstrate that current models face clear limitations in controllable style adaptation, highlighting both the novelty and challenge of this task. By releasing VStyle and its evaluation toolkit, we aim to provide the community with a foundation for advancing human centered spoken interaction. The dataset and code are publicly available at \\href{https://junzhan2000.github.io/VStyle.github.io/}{project's homepage}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09716","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:57Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:52:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09712","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09712","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09712","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Talha","familyName":"Tahir","name":"Tahir, Talha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Thinking Therapist: Training Large Language Models to Deliver Acceptance and Commitment Therapy using Supervised Fine-Tuning and Odds Ratio Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T02:30:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T21:31:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:39:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) is a third-wave cognitive behavioral therapy with emerging evidence of efficacy in several psychiatric conditions. This study investigates the impact of post-training methodology and explicit reasoning on the ability of a small open-weight large language model (LLM) to deliver ACT. Using synthetic ACT transcripts generated by Mistral-Large, we trained Llama-3.2-3b-Instruct with two distinct approaches, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and odds ratio policy optimization (ORPO), each with and without an explicit chain-of-thought (COT) reasoning step. Performance was evaluated by comparing these four post-trained variants against the base Instruct model. These models were benchmarked in simulated therapy sessions, with performance quantitatively assessed on the ACT Fidelity Measure (ACT-FM) and the Therapist Empathy Scale (TES) by an LLM judge that had been fine-tuned on human evaluations. Our findings demonstrate that the ORPO-trained models significantly outperformed both their SFT and Instruct counterparts on ACT fidelity ($χ^2(5) = 185.15, p \u0026lt; .001$) and therapeutic empathy ($χ^2(5) = 140.37, p \u0026lt; .001$). The effect of COT was conditional as it provided a significant benefit to SFT models, improving ACT-FM scores by an average of 2.68 points ($p \u0026lt; .001$), while offering no discernible advantage to the superior ORPO or instruct-tuned variants. We posit that the superiority of ORPO stems from its ability to learn the therapeutic `process' over imitating `content,' a key aspect of ACT, while COT acts as a necessary scaffold for models trained only via imitation. This study establishes that preference-aligned policy optimization can effectively instill ACT competencies in small LLMs, and that the utility of explicit reasoning is highly dependent on the underlying training paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09712","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:51Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:49:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10371","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10371","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10371","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seokjin","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Seokjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joongun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Joongun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spandan","familyName":"More","name":"More, Spandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanjiang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hanjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Jezghani","name":"Jezghani, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tushar","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Tushar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Divya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Characterizing the Efficiency of Distributed Training: A Power, Performance, and Thermal Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:05:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:43:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T14:28:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:50:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid scaling of Large Language Models (LLMs) has pushed training workloads far beyond the limits of single-node analysis, demanding a deeper understanding of how these models behave across large-scale, multi-GPU systems. In this paper, we present a comprehensive characterization of LLM training across diverse real-world workloads and hardware platforms, including NVIDIA H100/H200 and AMD MI250 GPUs. We analyze dense and sparse models under various parallelism strategies -- tensor, pipeline, data, and expert -- and evaluate their effects on hardware utilization, power consumption, and thermal behavior. We further evaluate the effectiveness of optimizations such as activation recomputation and compute-communication overlap. Our findings show that performance is not determined solely by scaling hardware capacity. Scale-up systems with fewer, higher-memory GPUs can outperform scale-out systems in communication-bound regimes, but only under carefully tuned configurations; in other cases, scale-out deployments achieve superior throughput. We also show that certain parallelism combinations, such as tensor with pipeline, lead to bandwidth underutilization due to inefficient data chunking, while increasing microbatch sizes beyond a certain point induces bursty execution and peak power excursions that worsen thermal throttling. These insights reveal how training performance is shaped by complex interactions between hardware, system topology, and model execution. We conclude by offering recommendations for system and hardware design to improve the scalability and reliability of future LLM systems and workloads. The source code of this project is available at https://github.com/sitar-lab/CharLLM-PPT."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10371","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:48:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bastián","familyName":"González-Bustamante","name":"González-Bustamante, Bastián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nando","familyName":"Verelst","name":"Verelst, Nando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carla","familyName":"Cisternas","name":"Cisternas, Carla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emulating Public Opinion: A Proof-of-Concept of AI-Generated Synthetic Survey Responses for the Chilean Case"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T50 (Primary) 91F10 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:43:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T00:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.17077752","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) offer promising avenues for methodological and applied innovations in survey research by using synthetic respondents to emulate human answers and behaviour, potentially mitigating measurement and representation errors. However, the extent to which LLMs recover aggregate item distributions remains uncertain and downstream applications risk reproducing social stereotypes and biases inherited from training data. We evaluate the reliability of LLM-generated synthetic survey responses against ground-truth human responses from a Chilean public opinion probabilistic survey. Specifically, we benchmark 128 prompt-model-question triplets, generating 189,696 synthetic profiles, and pool performance metrics (i.e., accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score) in a meta-analysis across 128 question-subsample pairs to test for biases along key sociodemographic dimensions. The evaluation spans OpenAI's GPT family and o-series reasoning models, as well as Llama and Qwen checkpoints. Three results stand out. First, synthetic responses achieve excellent performance on trust items (F1-score and accuracy \u0026gt; 0.90). Second, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini and Llama 4 Maverick perform comparably on this task. Third, synthetic-human alignment is highest among respondents aged 45-59. Overall, LLM-based synthetic samples approximate responses from a probabilistic sample, though with substantial item-level heterogeneity. Capturing the full nuance of public opinion remains challenging and requires careful calibration and additional distributional tests to ensure algorithmic fidelity and reduce errors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Working paper: 18 pages, 4 tables, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:36Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-22T02:43:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young-rok","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Young-rok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeongho","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Jeongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"SunYoung","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, SunYoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong-Hyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jong-Hyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younghyun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Younghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngjune","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Youngjune","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VARCO-VISION-2.0 Technical Report"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:55:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:21:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce VARCO-VISION-2.0, an open-weight bilingual vision-language model (VLM) for Korean and English with improved capabilities compared to the previous model VARCO-VISION-14B. The model supports multi-image understanding for complex inputs such as documents, charts, and tables, and delivers layoutaware OCR by predicting both textual content and its spatial location. Trained with a four-stage curriculum with memory-efficient techniques, the model achieves enhanced multimodal alignment, while preserving core language abilities and improving safety via preference optimization. Extensive benchmark evaluations demonstrate strong spatial grounding and competitive results for both languages, with the 14B model achieving 8th place on the OpenCompass VLM leaderboard among models of comparable scale. Alongside the 14B-scale model, we release a 1.7B version optimized for on-device deployment. We believe these models advance the development of bilingual VLMs and their practical applications. Two variants of VARCO-VISION-2.0 are available at Hugging Face: a full-scale 14B model and a lightweight 1.7B model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 1 figure, 14 tables. Technical report for VARCO-VISION-2.0, a Korean-English bilingual VLM in 14B and 1.7B variants. Key features: multi-image understanding, OCR with text localization, improved Korean capabilities"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:12Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:11:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Giovannini","name":"Giovannini, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Gemelli","name":"Gemelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Coppini","name":"Coppini, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Marinai","name":"Marinai, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Reliable and Interpretable Document Question Answering via VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T10:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:29:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T02:38:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:59:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have shown strong capabilities in document understanding, particularly in identifying and extracting textual information from complex documents. Despite this, accurately localizing answers within documents remains a major challenge, limiting both interpretability and real-world applicability. To address this, we introduce DocExplainerV0, a plug-and-play bounding-box prediction module that decouples answer generation from spatial localization. This design makes it applicable to existing VLMs, including proprietary systems where fine-tuning is not feasible. Through systematic evaluation, we provide quantitative insights into the gap between textual accuracy and spatial grounding, showing that correct answers often lack reliable localization. Our standardized framework highlights these shortcomings and establishes a benchmark for future research toward more interpretable and robust document information extraction VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:45Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:41:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Ormerod","name":"Ormerod, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gitit","familyName":"Kehat","name":"Kehat, Gitit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Long Context Automated Essay Scoring with Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T17:13:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:45:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformer-based language models are architecturally constrained to process text of a fixed maximum length. Essays written by higher-grade students frequently exceed the maximum allowed length for many popular open-source models. A common approach to addressing this issue when using these models for Automated Essay Scoring is to truncate the input text. This raises serious validity concerns as it undermines the model's ability to fully capture and evaluate organizational elements of the scoring rubric, which requires long contexts to assess. In this study, we evaluate several models that incorporate architectural modifications of the standard transformer architecture to overcome these length limitations using the Kaggle ASAP 2.0 dataset. The models considered in this study include fine-tuned versions of XLNet, Longformer, ModernBERT, Mamba, and Llama models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10396","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10396","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10396","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Siyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengchen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Miao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuandong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guan","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Guan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Bell","name":"Bell, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feiyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Feiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Inpainting-Guided Policy Optimization for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:44:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:44:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Masked diffusion large language models (dLLMs) are emerging as promising alternatives to autoregressive LLMs, offering competitive performance while supporting unique generation capabilities such as inpainting. We explore how inpainting can inform RL algorithm design for dLLMs. Aligning LLMs with reinforcement learning faces an exploration challenge: sparse reward signals and sample waste when models fail to discover correct solutions. While this inefficiency affects LLMs broadly, dLLMs offer a distinctive opportunity--their inpainting ability can guide exploration. We introduce IGPO (Inpainting Guided Policy Optimization), an RL framework that strategically inserts partial ground-truth reasoning traces during online sampling. Unlike providing full solutions, inpainting steers exploration toward promising trajectory spaces while preserving self-generated reasoning, bridging supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. We apply IGPO to group-based optimization methods such as GRPO, where exploration failures cause zero advantages and gradients. IGPO restores meaningful gradients while improving sample efficiency. We also propose supervised fine-tuning on synthetically rewritten concise traces that better align with dLLM generation patterns. With additional techniques including entropy-based filtering, our training recipe yields substantial gains across three mathematical benchmarks--GSM8K, Math500, and AMC--achieving new state-of-the-art results for full-attention masked dLLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint; 21 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10396","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:34:00Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:34:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:34:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10377","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10377","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10377","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongzhou","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Dongzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"zhiliang","familyName":"wu","name":"wu, zhiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hehe","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Hehe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dropping Experts, Recombining Neurons: Retraining-Free Pruning for Sparse Mixture-of-Experts LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:09:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:43:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Mixture-of-Experts (SMoE) architectures are widely used in large language models (LLMs) due to their computational efficiency. However, though only a few experts are activated for each token, SMoE still requires loading all expert parameters, leading to high memory usage and challenges in deployment. Previous work has tried to reduce the overhead by pruning and merging experts, but primarily focused on expert-level operations, leaving neuron-level structure underexplored. We propose DERN (Dropping Experts, Recombining Neurons), a task-agnostic and retraining-free framework for expert pruning and reconstruction. We observe that experts are often misaligned and contain semantic conflicts at the neuron level, which poses challenges for direct merging. To solve this, DERN works in three steps: it first prunes redundant experts using router statistics; then it decomposes them into neuron-level expert segments, assigning each segment to its most compatible retained expert; and finally, it merges segments within each retained expert to build a compact representation. Experiments on Mixtral, Qwen, and DeepSeek SMoE models show that DERN improves performance by more than 5% on commonsense reasoning and MMLU benchmarks under 50% expert sparsity, without extra training. It also greatly reduces the number of experts and memory usage, making SMoE LLMs easier to deploy in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10377","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10369","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10369","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10369","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gul Rukh","familyName":"Khattak","name":"Khattak, Gul Rukh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Patlatzoglou","name":"Patlatzoglou, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Barker","name":"Barker, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libor","familyName":"Pastika","name":"Pastika, Libor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boroumand","familyName":"Zeidaabadi","name":"Zeidaabadi, Boroumand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"El-Medany","name":"El-Medany, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hesham","familyName":"Aggour","name":"Aggour, Hesham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yixiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio H.","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Antonio H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Annis","name":"Annis, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio Luiz Pinho","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Antonio Luiz Pinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junbo","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Junbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel B.","familyName":"Kramer","name":"Kramer, Daniel B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan W.","familyName":"Waks","name":"Waks, Jonathan W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evan","familyName":"Brittain","name":"Brittain, Evan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Peters","name":"Peters, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fu Siong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Fu Siong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arunashis","familyName":"Sau","name":"Sau, Arunashis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data distribution impacts the performance and generalisability of contrastive learning-based foundation models of electrocardiograms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Tissues and Organs (q-bio.TO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T16:01:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:42:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive learning is a widely adopted self-supervised pretraining strategy, yet its dependence on cohort composition remains underexplored. We present Contrasting by Patient Augmented Electrocardiograms (CAPE) foundation model and pretrain on four cohorts (n = 5,203,352), from diverse populations across three continents (North America, South America, Asia). We systematically assess how cohort demographics, health status, and population diversity influence the downstream performance for prediction tasks also including two additional cohorts from another continent (Europe). We find that downstream performance depends on the distributional properties of the pretraining cohort, including demographics and health status. Moreover, while pretraining with a multi-centre, demographically diverse cohort improves in-distribution accuracy, it reduces out-of-distribution (OOD) generalisation of our contrastive approach by encoding cohort-specific artifacts. To address this, we propose the In-Distribution Batch (IDB) strategy, which preserves intra-cohort consistency during pretraining and enhances OOD robustness. This work provides important insights for developing clinically fair and generalisable foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Currently under review at npj Digital Medicine"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10369","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:23Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin David","familyName":"Shaffer","name":"Shaffer, Benjamin David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brooks","familyName":"Kinch","name":"Kinch, Brooks","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Klobusicky","name":"Klobusicky, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. Ani","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, M. Ani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathaniel","familyName":"Trask","name":"Trask, Nathaniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-informed sensor coverage through structure preserving machine learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:42:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a machine learning framework for adaptive source localization in which agents use a structure-preserving digital twin of a coupled hydrodynamic-transport system for real-time trajectory planning and data assimilation. The twin is constructed with conditional neural Whitney forms (CNWF), coupling the numerical guarantees of finite element exterior calculus (FEEC) with transformer-based operator learning. The resulting model preserves discrete conservation, and adapts in real time to streaming sensor data. It employs a conditional attention mechanism to identify: a reduced Whitney-form basis; reduced integral balance equations; and a source field, each compatible with given sensor measurements. The induced reduced-order environmental model retains the stability and consistency of standard finite-element simulation, yielding a physically realizable, regular mapping from sensor data to the source field. We propose a staggered scheme that alternates between evaluating the digital twin and applying Lloyd's algorithm to guide sensor placement, with analysis providing conditions for monotone improvement of a coverage functional. Using the predicted source field as an importance function within an optimal-recovery scheme, we demonstrate recovery of point sources under continuity assumptions, highlighting the role of regularity as a sufficient condition for localization. Experimental comparisons with physics-agnostic transformer architectures show improved accuracy in complex geometries when physical constraints are enforced, indicating that structure preservation provides an effective inductive bias for source identification."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:33:15Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:33:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:33:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10344","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10344","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10344","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexi","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuexi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicha C.","familyName":"Dvornek","name":"Dvornek, Nicha C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAM: Geometry-Guided Local Alignment for Multi-View VLP in Mammography"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T15:33:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:41:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mammography screening is an essential tool for early detection of breast cancer. The speed and accuracy of mammography interpretation have the potential to be improved with deep learning methods. However, the development of a foundation visual language model (VLM) is hindered by limited data and domain differences between natural and medical images. Existing mammography VLMs, adapted from natural images, often ignore domain-specific characteristics, such as multi-view relationships in mammography. Unlike radiologists who analyze both views together to process ipsilateral correspondence, current methods treat them as independent images or do not properly model the multi-view correspondence learning, losing critical geometric context and resulting in suboptimal prediction. We propose GLAM: Global and Local Alignment for Multi-view mammography for VLM pretraining using geometry guidance. By leveraging the prior knowledge about the multi-view imaging process of mammograms, our model learns local cross-view alignments and fine-grained local features through joint global and local, visual-visual, and visual-language contrastive learning. Pretrained on EMBED [14], one of the largest open mammography datasets, our model outperforms baselines across multiple datasets under different settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10344","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:32:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noga H.","familyName":"Rotman","name":"Rotman, Noga H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hila","familyName":"Peleg","name":"Peleg, Hila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Silberstein","name":"Silberstein, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RFSeek and Ye Shall Find"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T13:08:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Requests for Comments (RFCs) are extensive specification documents for network protocols, but their prose-based format and their considerable length often impede precise operational understanding. We present RFSeek, an interactive tool that automatically extracts visual summaries of protocol logic from RFCs. RFSeek leverages large language models (LLMs) to generate provenance-linked, explorable diagrams, surfacing both official state machines and additional logic found only in the RFC text. Compared to existing RFC visualizations, RFSeek's visual summaries are more transparent and easier to audit against their textual source. We showcase the tool's potential through a series of use cases, including guided knowledge extraction and semantic diffing, applied to protocols such as TCP, QUIC, PPTP, and DCCP.\n In practice, RFSeek not only reconstructs the RFC diagrams included in some specifications, but, more interestingly, also uncovers important logic such as nodes or edges described in the text but missing from those diagrams. RFSeek further derives new visualization diagrams for complex RFCs, with QUIC as a representative case. Our approach, which we term \\emph{Summary Visualization}, highlights a promising direction: combining LLMs with formal, user-customized visualizations to enhance protocol comprehension and support robust implementations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:47Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:29:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10208","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10208","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10208","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Shengqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SI-FACT: Mitigating Knowledge Conflict via Self-Improving Faithfulness-Aware Contrastive Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T12:56:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:34:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models often generate unfaithful responses in knowledge intensive tasks due to knowledge conflict,that is,a preference for relying on internal parametric knowledge rather than the provided context.To address this issue,we propose a novel self improving framework,Self Improving Faithfulness Aware Contrastive Tuning.The framework uses a self instruct mechanism that allows the base LLM to automatically generate high quality,structured contrastive learning data,including anchor samples,semantically equivalent positive samples,and negative samples simulating unfaithful scenarios.This approach significantly reduces the cost of manual annotation.Subsequently,contrastive learning is applied to train the model,enabling it to pull faithful responses closer and push unfaithful responses farther apart in the representation space.Experiments on knowledge conflict evaluation benchmarks ECARE KRE and COSE KRE show that the SI FACT model based on Llama3 8B Instruct improves the Contextual Recall Rate by 6.2% over the best baseline method,while significantly reducing dependence on internal memory.The results indicate that SI FACT provides strong effectiveness and high data efficiency in enhancing the contextual faithfulness of LLMs,offering a practical pathway toward building more proactive and trustworthy language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10208","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:29:36Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:29:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:29:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoshitaro","familyName":"Ohnishi","name":"Ohnishi, Hoshitaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideo","familyName":"Mukai","name":"Mukai, Hideo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Symmetry-Integrated Approach to Surface Code Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T11:41:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:31:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum error correction, which utilizes logical qubits that are encoded as redundant multiple physical qubits to find and correct errors in physical qubits, is indispensable for practical quantum computing. Surface code is considered to be a promising encoding method with a high error threshold that is defined by stabilizer generators. However, previous methods have suffered from the problem that the decoder acquires solely the error probability distribution because of the non-uniqueness of correct prediction obtained from the input. To circumvent this problem, we propose a technique to reoptimize the decoder model by approximating syndrome measurements with a continuous function that is mathematically interpolated by neural network. We evaluated the improvement in accuracy of a multilayer perceptron based decoder for code distances of 5 and 7 as well as for decoders based on convolutional and recurrent neural networks and transformers for a code distance of 5. In all cases, the reoptimized decoder gave better accuracy than the original models, demonstrating the universal effectiveness of the proposed method that is independent of code distance or network architecture. These results suggest that re-framing the problem of surface code decoding into a regression problem that can be tackled by deep learning is a useful strategy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:28:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riccardo","familyName":"Lunelli","name":"Lunelli, Riccardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angus","familyName":"Nicolson","name":"Nicolson, Angus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel Martin","familyName":"Pröll","name":"Pröll, Samuel Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian Johannes","familyName":"Reinstadler","name":"Reinstadler, Sebastian Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clemens","familyName":"Dlaska","name":"Dlaska, Clemens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BenchECG and xECG: a benchmark and baseline for ECG foundation models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T11:27:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:30:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Electrocardiograms (ECGs) are inexpensive, widely used, and well-suited to deep learning. Recently, interest has grown in developing foundation models for ECGs - models that generalise across diverse downstream tasks. However, consistent evaluation has been lacking: prior work often uses narrow task selections and inconsistent datasets, hindering fair comparison. Here, we introduce BenchECG, a standardised benchmark comprising a comprehensive suite of publicly available ECG datasets and versatile tasks. We also propose xECG, an xLSTM-based recurrent model trained with SimDINOv2 self-supervised learning, which achieves the best BenchECG score compared to publicly available state-of-the-art models. In particular, xECG is the only publicly available model to perform strongly on all datasets and tasks. By standardising evaluation, BenchECG enables rigorous comparison and aims to accelerate progress in ECG representation learning. xECG achieves superior performance over earlier approaches, defining a new baseline for future ECG foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 4 figures, 22 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:28:17Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:28:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:28:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Linke","name":"Linke, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barbara","familyName":"Schuppler","name":"Schuppler, Barbara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prominence-aware automatic speech recognition for conversational speech"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T10:18:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:28:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates prominence-aware automatic speech recognition (ASR) by combining prominence detection and speech recognition for conversational Austrian German. First, prominence detectors were developed by fine-tuning wav2vec2 models to classify word-level prominence. The detector was then used to automatically annotate prosodic prominence in a large corpus. Based on those annotations, we trained novel prominence-aware ASR systems that simultaneously transcribe words and their prominence levels. The integration of prominence information did not change performance compared to our baseline ASR system, while reaching a prominence detection accuracy of 85.53% for utterances where the recognized word sequence was correct. This paper shows that transformer-based models can effectively encode prosodic information and represents a novel contribution to prosody-enhanced ASR, with potential applications for linguistic research and prosody-informed dialogue systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:27Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:27:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulrahman","familyName":"Allam","name":"Allam, Abdulrahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Seif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hamdi","name":"Hamdi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khaled","familyName":"Shaban","name":"Shaban, Khaled","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Arabic Medical Chatbots Using Synthetic Data: Enhancing Generative AI with Synthetic Patient Records"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:58:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:27:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The development of medical chatbots in Arabic is significantly constrained by the scarcity of large-scale, high-quality annotated datasets. While prior efforts compiled a dataset of 20,000 Arabic patient-doctor interactions from social media to fine-tune large language models (LLMs), model scalability and generalization remained limited. In this study, we propose a scalable synthetic data augmentation strategy to expand the training corpus to 100,000 records. Using advanced generative AI systems ChatGPT-4o and Gemini 2.5 Pro we generated 80,000 contextually relevant and medically coherent synthetic question-answer pairs grounded in the structure of the original dataset. These synthetic samples were semantically filtered, manually validated, and integrated into the training pipeline. We fine-tuned five LLMs, including Mistral-7B and AraGPT2, and evaluated their performance using BERTScore metrics and expert-driven qualitative assessments. To further analyze the effectiveness of synthetic sources, we conducted an ablation study comparing ChatGPT-4o and Gemini-generated data independently. The results showed that ChatGPT-4o data consistently led to higher F1-scores and fewer hallucinations across all models. Overall, our findings demonstrate the viability of synthetic augmentation as a practical solution for enhancing domain-specific language models in-low resource medical NLP, paving the way for more inclusive, scalable, and accurate Arabic healthcare chatbot systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in AICCSA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:27:16Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:27:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:27:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulrahman","familyName":"Allam","name":"Allam, Abdulrahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Seif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hamdi","name":"Hamdi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ammar","familyName":"Mohammed","name":"Mohammed, Ammar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Arabic Large Language Models for Medical Text Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:37:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:26:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Efficient hospital management systems (HMS) are critical worldwide to address challenges such as overcrowding, limited resources, and poor availability of urgent health care. Existing methods often lack the ability to provide accurate, real-time medical advice, particularly for irregular inputs and underrepresented languages. To overcome these limitations, this study proposes an approach that fine-tunes large language models (LLMs) for Arabic medical text generation. The system is designed to assist patients by providing accurate medical advice, diagnoses, drug recommendations, and treatment plans based on user input. The research methodology required the collection of a unique dataset from social media platforms, capturing real-world medical conversations between patients and doctors. The dataset, which includes patient complaints together with medical advice, was properly cleaned and preprocessed to account for multiple Arabic dialects. Fine-tuning state-of-the-art generative models, such as Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, LLaMA-2-7B, and GPT-2 Medium, optimized the system's ability to generate reliable medical text. Results from evaluations indicate that the fine-tuned Mistral-7B model outperformed the other models, achieving average BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) Score values in precision, recall, and F1-scores of 68.5\\%, 69.08\\%, and 68.5\\%, respectively. Comparative benchmarking and qualitative assessments validate the system's ability to produce coherent and relevant medical replies to informal input. This study highlights the potential of generative artificial intelligence (AI) in advancing HMS, offering a scalable and adaptable solution for global healthcare challenges, especially in linguistically and culturally diverse environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in 2025 4th International Conference on Computer Technologies (ICCTech)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:58Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:26:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Adamu","name":"Adamu, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huitong","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Huitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longin Jan","familyName":"Latecki","name":"Latecki, Longin Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduard C.","familyName":"Dragut","name":"Dragut, Eduard C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Querying Climate Knowledge: Semantic Retrieval for Scientific Discovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T09:28:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing complexity and volume of climate science literature make it increasingly difficult for researchers to find relevant information across models, datasets, regions, and variables. This paper introduces a domain-specific Knowledge Graph (KG) built from climate publications and broader scientific texts, aimed at improving how climate knowledge is accessed and used. Unlike keyword based search, our KG supports structured, semantic queries that help researchers discover precise connections such as which models have been validated in specific regions or which datasets are commonly used with certain teleconnection patterns. We demonstrate how the KG answers such questions using Cypher queries, and outline its integration with large language models in RAG systems to improve transparency and reliability in climate-related question answering. This work moves beyond KG construction to show its real world value for climate researchers, model developers, and others who rely on accurate, contextual scientific information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM SIGIR 2025 Workshop MANILA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:26:47Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:26:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:26:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Madhushan","familyName":"Ramalingam","name":"Ramalingam, Madhushan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncertainty-Aware Tabular Prediction: Evaluating VBLL-Enhanced TabPFN in Safety-Critical Medical Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T08:24:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:23:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predictive models are being increasingly used across a wide range of domains, including safety-critical applications such as medical diagnosis and criminal justice. Reliable uncertainty estimation is a crucial task in such settings. Tabular Prior-data Fitted Network (TabPFN) is a recently proposed machine learning foundation model for tabular dataset, which uses a generative transformer architecture. Variational Bayesian Last Layers (VBLL) is a state-of-the-art lightweight variational formulation that effectively improves uncertainty estimation with minimal computational overhead. In this work we aim to evaluate the performance of VBLL integrated with the recently proposed TabPFN in uncertainty calibration. Our experiments, conducted on three benchmark medical tabular datasets, compare the performance of the original TabPFN and the VBLL-integrated version. Contrary to expectations, we observed that original TabPFN consistently outperforms VBLL integrated TabPFN in uncertainty calibration across all datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:25:51Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:25:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:25:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Basem","name":"Basem, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Younes","name":"Younes, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seif","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Seif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrahman","familyName":"Moustafa","name":"Moustafa, Abdelrahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"!MSA at BAREC Shared Task 2025: Ensembling Arabic Transformers for Readability Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T08:08:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:22:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present MSAs winning system for the BAREC 2025 Shared Task on fine-grained Arabic readability assessment, achieving first place in six of six tracks. Our approach is a confidence-weighted ensemble of four complementary transformer models (AraBERTv2, AraELECTRA, MARBERT, and CAMeLBERT) each fine-tuned with distinct loss functions to capture diverse readability signals. To tackle severe class imbalance and data scarcity, we applied weighted training, advanced preprocessing, SAMER corpus relabeling with our strongest model, and synthetic data generation via Gemini 2.5 Flash, adding about 10,000 rare-level samples. A targeted post-processing step corrected prediction distribution skew, delivering a 6.3 percent Quadratic Weighted Kappa (QWK) gain. Our system reached 87.5 percent QWK at the sentence level and 87.4 percent at the document level, demonstrating the power of model and loss diversity, confidence-informed fusion, and intelligent augmentation for robust Arabic readability prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 Pages , 8 figures , ArabicNLP 2025 , Co-located with EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:25:40Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:25:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:25:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Strahinja","familyName":"Nikolic","name":"Nikolic, Strahinja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilker","familyName":"Oguz","name":"Oguz, Ilker","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Demetri","familyName":"Psaltis","name":"Psaltis, Demetri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Expert Specialization through Unsupervised Training in Sparse Mixture of Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T07:45:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:22:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the internal organization of neural networks remains a fundamental challenge in deep learning interpretability. We address this challenge by exploring a novel Sparse Mixture of Experts Variational Autoencoder (SMoE-VAE) architecture. We test our model on the QuickDraw dataset, comparing unsupervised expert routing against a supervised baseline guided by ground-truth labels. Surprisingly, we find that unsupervised routing consistently achieves superior reconstruction performance. The experts learn to identify meaningful sub-categorical structures that often transcend human-defined class boundaries. Through t-SNE visualizations and reconstruction analysis, we investigate how MoE models uncover fundamental data structures that are more aligned with the model's objective than predefined labels. Furthermore, our study on the impact of dataset size provides insights into the trade-offs between data quantity and expert specialization, offering guidance for designing efficient MoE architectures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:25:17Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:25:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:25:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan","familyName":"Ahmad","name":"Ahmad, Adnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philine","familyName":"Kowol","name":"Kowol, Philine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Hillmann","name":"Hillmann, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Möller","name":"Möller, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Intent Recognition in Dialogue Understanding: A Comparison Between Smaller Open-Source LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T07:10:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we provide an extensive analysis of multi-label intent classification using Large Language Models (LLMs) that are open-source, publicly available, and can be run in consumer hardware. We use the MultiWOZ 2.1 dataset, a benchmark in the dialogue system domain, to investigate the efficacy of three popular open-source pre-trained LLMs, namely LLama2-7B-hf, Mistral-7B-v0.1, and Yi-6B. We perform the classification task in a few-shot setup, giving 20 examples in the prompt with some instructions. Our approach focuses on the differences in performance of these models across several performance metrics by methodically assessing these models on multi-label intent classification tasks. Additionally, we compare the performance of the instruction-based fine-tuning approach with supervised learning using the smaller transformer model BertForSequenceClassification as a baseline. To evaluate the performance of the models, we use evaluation metrics like accuracy, precision, and recall as well as micro, macro, and weighted F1 score. We also report the inference time, VRAM requirements, etc. The Mistral-7B-v0.1 outperforms two other generative models on 11 intent classes out of 14 in terms of F-Score, with a weighted average of 0.50. It also has relatively lower Humming Loss and higher Jaccard Similarity, making it the winning model in the few-shot setting. We find BERT based supervised classifier having superior performance compared to the best performing few-shot generative LLM. The study provides a framework for small open-source LLMs in detecting complex multi-intent dialogues, enhancing the Natural Language Understanding aspect of task-oriented chatbots."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:24:54Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:24:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ponhvoan","familyName":"Srey","name":"Srey, Ponhvoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anh Tuan","familyName":"Luu","name":"Luu, Anh Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Hallucination Detection by Inspecting Reasoning Processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T06:58:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unsupervised hallucination detection aims to identify hallucinated content generated by large language models (LLMs) without relying on labeled data. While unsupervised methods have gained popularity by eliminating labor-intensive human annotations, they frequently rely on proxy signals unrelated to factual correctness. This misalignment biases detection probes toward superficial or non-truth-related aspects, limiting generalizability across datasets and scenarios. To overcome these limitations, we propose IRIS, an unsupervised hallucination detection framework, leveraging internal representations intrinsic to factual correctness. IRIS prompts the LLM to carefully verify the truthfulness of a given statement, and obtain its contextualized embedding as informative features for training. Meanwhile, the uncertainty of each response is considered a soft pseudolabel for truthfulness. Experimental results demonstrate that IRIS consistently outperforms existing unsupervised methods. Our approach is fully unsupervised, computationally low cost, and works well even with few training data, making it suitable for real-time detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:24:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhitian","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanli","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Nanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyong","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Tianyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Meet Legal Artificial Intelligence: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T05:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:16:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have significantly advanced the development of Legal Artificial Intelligence (Legal AI) in recent years, enhancing the efficiency and accuracy of legal tasks. To advance research and applications of LLM-based approaches in legal domain, this paper provides a comprehensive review of 16 legal LLMs series and 47 LLM-based frameworks for legal tasks, and also gather 15 benchmarks and 29 datasets to evaluate different legal capabilities. Additionally, we analyse the challenges and discuss future directions for LLM-based approaches in the legal domain. We hope this paper provides a systematic introduction for beginners and encourages future research in this field. Resources are available at https://github.com/ZhitianHou/LLMs4LegalAI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:23:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09955","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09955","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09955","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Erak","name":"Erak, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Alhussein","name":"Alhussein, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatem","familyName":"Abou-Zeid","name":"Abou-Zeid, Hatem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehdi","familyName":"Bennis","name":"Bennis, Mehdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami","familyName":"Muhaidat","name":"Muhaidat, Sami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Token Merging for Efficient Transformer Semantic Communication at the Edge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T04:11:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:14:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale transformers are central to modern semantic communication, yet their high computational and communication costs hinder deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. This paper introduces a training-free framework for adaptive token merging, a novel mechanism that compresses transformer representations at runtime by selectively merging semantically redundant tokens under per-layer similarity thresholds. Unlike prior fixed-ratio reduction, our approach couples merging directly to input redundancy, enabling data-dependent adaptation that balances efficiency and task relevance without retraining. We cast the discovery of merging strategies as a multi-objective optimization problem and leverage Bayesian optimization to obtain Pareto-optimal trade-offs between accuracy, inference cost, and communication cost. On ImageNet classification, we match the accuracy of the unmodified transformer with 30\\% fewer floating-point operations per second and under 20\\% of the original communication cost, while for visual question answering our method achieves performance competitive with the full LLaVA model at less than one-third of the compute and one-tenth of the bandwidth. Finally, we show that our adaptive merging is robust across varying channel conditions and provides inherent privacy benefits, substantially degrading the efficacy of model inversion attacks. Our framework provides a practical and versatile solution for deploying powerful transformer models in resource-limited edge intelligence scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE Journals"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09955","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:23:35Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:23:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:23:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yago Romano","familyName":"Matinez","name":"Matinez, Yago Romano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Roberts","name":"Roberts, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs as Agentic Cooperative Players in Multiplayer UNO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:07:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs promise to assist humans -- not just by answering questions, but by offering useful guidance across a wide range of tasks. But how far does that assistance go? Can a large language model based agent actually help someone accomplish their goal as an active participant? We test this question by engaging an LLM in UNO, a turn-based card game, asking it not to win but instead help another player to do so. We built a tool that allows decoder-only LLMs to participate as agents within the RLCard game environment. These models receive full game-state information and respond using simple text prompts under two distinct prompting strategies. We evaluate models ranging from small (1B parameters) to large (70B parameters) and explore how model scale impacts performance. We find that while all models were able to successfully outperform a random baseline when playing UNO, few were able to significantly aid another player."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jenny Y.","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jenny Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehul","familyName":"Damani","name":"Damani, Mehul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousef","familyName":"El-Kurdi","name":"El-Kurdi, Yousef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramon","familyName":"Astudillo","name":"Astudillo, Ramon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latency and Token-Aware Test-Time Compute"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:35:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:07:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference-time scaling has emerged as a powerful way to improve large language model (LLM) performance by generating multiple candidate responses and selecting among them. However, existing work on dynamic allocation for test-time compute typically considers only parallel generation methods such as best-of-N, overlooking incremental decoding methods like beam search, and has largely ignored latency, focusing only on token usage. We formulate inference-time scaling as a problem of dynamic compute allocation and method selection, where the system must decide which strategy to apply and how much compute to allocate on a per-query basis. Our framework explicitly incorporates both token cost and wall-clock latency, the latter being critical for user experience and particularly for agentic workflows where models must issue multiple queries efficiently. Experiments on reasoning benchmarks show that our approach consistently outperforms static strategies, achieving favorable accuracy-cost trade-offs while remaining practical for deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Austin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Austin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Topic-Guided Reinforcement Learning with LLMs for Enhancing Multi-Document Summarization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T21:01:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:06:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key challenge in Multi-Document Summarization (MDS) is effectively integrating information from multiple sources while maintaining coherence and topical relevance. While Large Language Models have shown impressive results in single-document summarization, their performance on MDS still leaves room for improvement. In this paper, we propose a topic-guided reinforcement learning approach to improve content selection in MDS. We first show that explicitly prompting models with topic labels enhances the informativeness of the generated summaries. Building on this insight, we propose a novel topic reward within the Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) framework to measure topic alignment between the generated summary and source documents. Experimental results on the Multi-News and Multi-XScience datasets demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms strong baselines, highlighting the effectiveness of leveraging topical cues in MDS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:21:09Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:21:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:21:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09801","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09801","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09801","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brennen","familyName":"Hill","name":"Hill, Brennen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HEFT: A Coarse-to-Fine Hierarchy for Enhancing the Efficiency and Accuracy of Language Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6; C.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T50, 68T05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T19:06:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:04:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The adaptation of large language models (LLMs) to specialized reasoning tasks is fundamentally constrained by computational resources. Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods have emerged as a powerful solution, yet the landscape of these techniques is diverse, with distinct methods operating in either the model's weight space or its representation space. This paper investigates the hypothesis that a synergistic combination of these paradigms can unlock superior performance and efficiency. We introduce HEFT (Hierarchical Efficient Fine-Tuning), a novel hierarchical adaptation strategy that composes two distinct PEFT methods in a coarse-to-fine manner: first, a broad, foundational adaptation in the weight space using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), followed by a precise, surgical refinement of internal activations using Representation Fine-Tuning (ReFT). We evaluate this approach by fine-tuning a Llama-2-7B model on the BoolQ benchmark, a challenging dataset for inferential reasoning. Our results reveal a profound synergistic effect. A model fine-tuned for only three epochs with our HEFT strategy achieves an accuracy of 85.17\\%, exceeding the performance of models trained for 20 epochs with either LoRA-only (85.05\\%) or ReFT-only (83.36\\%) methodologies. This work demonstrates that the thoughtful composition of PEFT methods is a potent algorithmic innovation, offering a more efficient and effective path toward advancing the reasoning capabilities of language models. By achieving superior results with a fraction of the computational budget, our findings present a principled approach to overcoming the obstacles inherent in adapting large-scale models for complex cognitive tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09801","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:19:56Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:19:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:19:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09782","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09782","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09782","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshini","familyName":"Pulishetty","name":"Pulishetty, Roshini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mani Kishan","familyName":"Ghantasala","name":"Ghantasala, Mani Kishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keerthy Kaushik","familyName":"Dasoju","name":"Dasoju, Keerthy Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niti","familyName":"Mangwani","name":"Mangwani, Niti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishal","familyName":"Garimella","name":"Garimella, Vishal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Mate","name":"Mate, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somya","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Somya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehi","familyName":"Nosakhare","name":"Nosakhare, Ehi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadid","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Sadid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soundar","familyName":"Srinivasan","name":"Srinivasan, Soundar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"One Head, Many Models: Cross-Attention Routing for Cost-Aware LLM Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T18:29:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:02:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of large language models (LLMs) with varying computational costs and performance profiles presents a critical challenge for scalable, cost-effective deployment in real-world applications. We introduce a unified routing framework that leverages a single-head cross-attention mechanism to jointly model query and model embeddings, enabling dynamic selection of the optimal LLM for each input query. Our approach is evaluated on RouterBench, a large-scale, publicly available benchmark encompassing diverse LLM pools and domains. By explicitly capturing fine-grained query-model interactions, our router predicts both response quality and generation cost, achieving up to 6.6% improvement in Average Improvement in Quality (AIQ) and 2.9% in maximum performance over existing routers. To robustly balance performance and cost, we propose an exponential reward function that enhances stability across user preferences. The resulting architecture is lightweight, generalizes effectively across domains, and demonstrates improved efficiency compared to prior methods, establishing a new standard for cost-aware LLM routing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09782","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:19:29Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:19:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:19:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqun","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Song","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Song","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengze","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daxin","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Daxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Cam-Tu","name":"Cam-Tu, Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAVa: Layer-wise KV Cache Eviction with Dynamic Budget Allocation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-11T16:48:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV Cache is commonly used to accelerate LLM inference with long contexts, yet its high memory demand drives the need for cache compression. Existing compression methods, however, are largely heuristic and lack dynamic budget allocation. To address this limitation, we introduce a unified framework for cache compression by minimizing information loss in Transformer residual streams. Building on it, we analyze the layer attention output loss and derive a new metric to compare cache entries across heads, enabling layer-wise compression with dynamic head budgets. Additionally, by contrasting cross-layer information, we also achieve dynamic layer budgets. LAVa is the first unified strategy for cache eviction and dynamic budget allocation that, unlike prior methods, does not rely on training or the combination of multiple strategies. Experiments with benchmarks (LongBench, Needle-In-A-Haystack, Ruler, and InfiniteBench) demonstrate its superiority. Moreover, our experiments reveal a new insight: dynamic layer budgets are crucial for generation tasks (e.g., code completion), while dynamic head budgets play a key role in extraction tasks (e.g., extractive QA). As a fully dynamic compression method, LAVa consistently maintains top performance across task types. Our code is available at https://github.com/MGDDestiny/Lava."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing-Yue Monica","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Xing-Yue Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron Archer","familyName":"Waterman","name":"Waterman, Aaron Archer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommaso","familyName":"Biancalani","name":"Biancalani, Tommaso","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Richmond","name":"Richmond, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yogesh","familyName":"Pandit","name":"Pandit, Yogesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avtar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Avtar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Russell","familyName":"Littman","name":"Littman, Russell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan-Christian","familyName":"Huetter","name":"Huetter, Jan-Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Ermakov","name":"Ermakov, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HypoGeneAgent: A Hypothesis Language Agent for Gene-Set Cluster Resolution Selection Using Perturb-seq Datasets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T22:25:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale single-cell and Perturb-seq investigations routinely involve clustering cells and subsequently annotating each cluster with Gene-Ontology (GO) terms to elucidate the underlying biological programs. However, both stages, resolution selection and functional annotation, are inherently subjective, relying on heuristics and expert curation. We present HYPOGENEAGENT, a large language model (LLM)-driven framework, transforming cluster annotation into a quantitatively optimizable task. Initially, an LLM functioning as a gene-set analyst analyzes the content of each gene program or perturbation module and generates a ranked list of GO-based hypotheses, accompanied by calibrated confidence scores. Subsequently, we embed every predicted description with a sentence-embedding model, compute pair-wise cosine similarities, and let the agent referee panel score (i) the internal consistency of the predictions, high average similarity within the same cluster, termed intra-cluster agreement (ii) their external distinctiveness, low similarity between clusters, termed inter-cluster separation. These two quantities are combined to produce an agent-derived resolution score, which is maximized when clusters exhibit simultaneous coherence and mutual exclusivity. When applied to a public K562 CRISPRi Perturb-seq dataset as a preliminary test, our Resolution Score selects clustering granularities that exhibit alignment with known pathway compared to classical metrics such silhouette score, modularity score for gene functional enrichment summary. These findings establish LLM agents as objective adjudicators of cluster resolution and functional annotation, thereby paving the way for fully automated, context-aware interpretation pipelines in single-cell multi-omics studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:30Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09737","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09737","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09737","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klemen","familyName":"Kotar","name":"Kotar, Klemen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanhee","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wanhee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Venkatesh","name":"Venkatesh, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Bear","name":"Bear, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Watrous","name":"Watrous, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khai Loong","familyName":"Aw","name":"Aw, Khai Loong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lilian Naing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lilian Naing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Stojanov","name":"Stojanov, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Feigelis","name":"Feigelis, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Thobani","name":"Thobani, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Durango","name":"Durango, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khaled","familyName":"Jedoui","name":"Jedoui, Khaled","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atlas","familyName":"Kazemian","name":"Kazemian, Atlas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Yamins","name":"Yamins, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"World Modeling with Probabilistic Structure Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T18:01:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Probabilistic Structure Integration (PSI), a system for learning richly controllable and flexibly promptable world models from data. PSI consists of a three-step cycle. The first step, Probabilistic prediction, involves building a probabilistic graphical model Psi of the data, in the form of a random-access autoregressive sequence model. Psi supports a complete set of learned conditional distributions describing the dependence of any variables in the data on any other set of variables. In step 2, Structure extraction, we show how to extract underlying low-dimensional properties in the data, corresponding to a diverse set of meaningful \"intermediate structures\", in a zero-shot fashion via causal inference on Psi. Step 3, Integration, completes the cycle by converting these structures into new token types that are then continually mixed back into the training diet as conditioning signals and prediction targets. Each such cycle augments the capabilities of Psi, both allowing it to model the underlying data better, and creating new control handles -- akin to an LLM-like universal prompting language. We train an instance of Psi on 1.4 trillion tokens of internet video data; we use it to perform a variety of useful video prediction and understanding inferences; we extract state-of-the-art optical flow, self-supervised depth and object segmentation; and we use these structures to support a full cycle of predictive improvements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09737","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:26Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09735","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09735","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09735","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willem","familyName":"Huijzer","name":"Huijzer, Willem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jieying","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jieying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrimination by LLMs: Cross-lingual Bias Assessment and Mitigation in Decision-Making and Summarisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T14:25:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into various domains raises concerns about societal inequalities and information bias. This study examines biases in LLMs related to background, gender, and age, with a focus on their impact on decision-making and summarization tasks. Additionally, the research examines the cross-lingual propagation of these biases and evaluates the effectiveness of prompt-instructed mitigation strategies. Using an adapted version of the dataset by Tamkin et al. (2023) translated into Dutch, we created 151,200 unique prompts for the decision task and 176,400 for the summarisation task. Various demographic variables, instructions, salience levels, and languages were tested on GPT-3.5 and GPT-4o. Our analysis revealed that both models were significantly biased during decision-making, favouring female gender, younger ages, and certain backgrounds such as the African-American background. In contrast, the summarisation task showed minimal evidence of bias, though significant age-related differences emerged for GPT-3.5 in English. Cross-lingual analysis showed that bias patterns were broadly similar between English and Dutch, though notable differences were observed across specific demographic categories. The newly proposed mitigation instructions, while unable to eliminate biases completely, demonstrated potential in reducing them. The most effective instruction achieved a 27\\% mean reduction in the gap between the most and least favorable demographics. Notably, contrary to GPT-3.5, GPT-4o displayed reduced biases for all prompts in English, indicating the specific potential for prompt-based mitigation within newer models. This research underscores the importance of cautious adoption of LLMs and context-specific bias testing, highlighting the need for continued development of effective mitigation strategies to ensure responsible deployment of AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09735","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:23Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09731","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09731","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09731","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Haiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuecheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xuecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guozhi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Guozhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An-Lan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, An-Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanshen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hanshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangfan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yangfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanhuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Quanhuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingqun","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jingqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Vision-Language Models on Chinese Ancient Documents: From OCR to Knowledge Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T13:02:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Chinese ancient documents, invaluable carriers of millennia of Chinese history and culture, hold rich knowledge across diverse fields but face challenges in digitization and understanding, i.e., traditional methods only scan images, while current Vision-Language Models (VLMs) struggle with their visual and linguistic complexity. Existing document benchmarks focus on English printed texts or simplified Chinese, leaving a gap for evaluating VLMs on ancient Chinese documents. To address this, we present AncientDoc, the first benchmark for Chinese ancient documents, designed to assess VLMs from OCR to knowledge reasoning. AncientDoc includes five tasks (page-level OCR, vernacular translation, reasoning-based QA, knowledge-based QA, linguistic variant QA) and covers 14 document types, over 100 books, and about 3,000 pages. Based on AncientDoc, we evaluate mainstream VLMs using multiple metrics, supplemented by a human-aligned large language model for scoring."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09731","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:18Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Rohde","name":"Rohde, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Klingwort","name":"Klingwort, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Borgs","name":"Borgs, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A meta-analysis on the performance of machine-learning based language models for sentiment analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T10:05:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a meta-analysis evaluating ML performance in sentiment analysis for Twitter data. The study aims to estimate the average performance, assess heterogeneity between and within studies, and analyze how study characteristics influence model performance. Using PRISMA guidelines, we searched academic databases and selected 195 trials from 20 studies with 12 study features. Overall accuracy, the most reported performance metric, was analyzed using double arcsine transformation and a three-level random effects model. The average overall accuracy of the AIC-optimized model was 0.80 [0.76, 0.84]. This paper provides two key insights: 1) Overall accuracy is widely used but often misleading due to its sensitivity to class imbalance and the number of sentiment classes, highlighting the need for normalization. 2) Standardized reporting of model performance, including reporting confusion matrices for independent test sets, is essential for reliable comparisons of ML classifiers across studies, which seems far from common practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:14Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjun","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Role-Aware Multi-Agent Framework for Financial Education Question Answering with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T09:40:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Question answering (QA) plays a central role in financial education, yet existing large language model (LLM) approaches often fail to capture the nuanced and specialized reasoning required for financial problem-solving. The financial domain demands multistep quantitative reasoning, familiarity with domain-specific terminology, and comprehension of real-world scenarios. We present a multi-agent framework that leverages role-based prompting to enhance performance on domain-specific QA. Our framework comprises a Base Generator, an Evidence Retriever, and an Expert Reviewer agent that work in a single-pass iteration to produce a refined answer. We evaluated our framework on a set of 3,532 expert-designed finance education questions from Study.com, an online learning platform. We leverage retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) for contextual evidence from 6 finance textbooks and prompting strategies for a domain-expert reviewer. Our experiments indicate that critique-based refinement improves answer accuracy by 6.6-8.3% over zero-shot Chain-of-Thought baselines, with the highest performance from Gemini-2.0-Flash. Furthermore, our method enables GPT-4o-mini to achieve performance comparable to the finance-tuned FinGPT-mt_Llama3-8B_LoRA. Our results show a cost-effective approach to enhancing financial QA and offer insights for further research in multi-agent financial LLM systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 figures, Underreview"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:13Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09726","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09726","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09726","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seiji","familyName":"Hattori","name":"Hattori, Seiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuya","familyName":"Matsuzaki","name":"Matsuzaki, Takuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Makoto","familyName":"Fujiwara","name":"Fujiwara, Makoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Natural Language Translation of Formal Proofs through Informalization of Proof Steps and Recursive Summarization along Proof Structure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T09:22:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a natural language translation method for machine-verifiable formal proofs that leverages the informalization (verbalization of formal language proof steps) and summarization capabilities of LLMs. For evaluation, it was applied to formal proof data created in accordance with natural language proofs taken from an undergraduate-level textbook, and the quality of the generated natural language proofs was analyzed in comparison with the original natural language proofs. Furthermore, we will demonstrate that this method can output highly readable and accurate natural language proofs by applying it to existing formal proof library of the Lean proof assistant."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to INLG 2025 (accepted)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09726","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:11Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09724","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09724","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09724","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Wonyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujeong","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Sujeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juhyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Juhyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiTTO-LLM: Framework for Discovering Topic-based Technology Opportunities via Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68T09","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T05:47:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Technology opportunities are critical information that serve as a foundation for advancements in technology, industry, and innovation. This paper proposes a framework based on the temporal relationships between technologies to identify emerging technology opportunities. The proposed framework begins by extracting text from a patent dataset, followed by mapping text-based topics to discover inter-technology relationships. Technology opportunities are then identified by tracking changes in these topics over time. To enhance efficiency, the framework leverages a large language model to extract topics and employs a prompt for a chat-based language model to support the discovery of technology opportunities. The framework was evaluated using an artificial intelligence patent dataset provided by the United States Patent and Trademark Office. The experimental results suggest that artificial intelligence technology is evolving into forms that facilitate everyday accessibility. This approach demonstrates the potential of the proposed framework to identify future technology opportunities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09724","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:08Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09722","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09722","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09722","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Archibald","name":"Archibald, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving MLLM Historical Record Extraction with Test-Time Image"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T03:18:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel ensemble framework that stabilizes LLM based text extraction from noisy historical documents. We transcribe multiple augmented variants of each image with Gemini 2.0 Flash and fuse these outputs with a custom Needleman Wunsch style aligner that yields both a consensus transcription and a confidence score. We present a new dataset of 622 Pennsylvania death records, and demonstrate our method improves transcription accuracy by 4 percentage points relative to a single shot baseline. We find that padding and blurring are the most useful for improving accuracy, while grid warp perturbations are best for separating high and low confidence cases. The approach is simple, scalable, and immediately deployable to other document collections and transcription models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09722","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:05Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09721","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09721","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09721","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingxin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dingxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal RAG Framework for Housing Damage Assessment: Collaborative Optimization of Image Encoding and Policy Vector Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T01:58:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"After natural disasters, accurate evaluations of damage to housing are important for insurance claims response and planning of resources. In this work, we introduce a novel multimodal retrieval-augmented generation (MM-RAG) framework. On top of classical RAG architecture, we further the framework to devise a two-branch multimodal encoder structure that the image branch employs a visual encoder composed of ResNet and Transformer to extract the characteristic of building damage after disaster, and the text branch harnesses a BERT retriever for the text vectorization of posts as well as insurance policies and for the construction of a retrievable restoration index. To impose cross-modal semantic alignment, the model integrates a cross-modal interaction module to bridge the semantic representation between image and text via multi-head attention. Meanwhile, in the generation module, the introduced modal attention gating mechanism dynamically controls the role of visual evidence and text prior information during generation. The entire framework takes end-to-end training, and combines the comparison loss, the retrieval loss and the generation loss to form multi-task optimization objectives, and achieves image understanding and policy matching in collaborative learning. The results demonstrate superior performance in retrieval accuracy and classification index on damage severity, where the Top-1 retrieval accuracy has been improved by 9.6%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09721","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:18:04Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:18:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:18:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09715","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09715","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09715","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naveen","familyName":"Lamba","name":"Lamba, Naveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanju","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Sanju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manas","familyName":"Gaur","name":"Gaur, Manas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating Symbolic Triggers of Hallucination in Gemma Models Across HaluEval and TruthfulQA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T05:50:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucination in Large Language Models (LLMs) is a well studied problem. However, the properties that make LLM intrinsically vulnerable to hallucinations have not been identified and studied. This research identifies and characterizes the key properties, allowing us to pinpoint vulnerabilities within the model's internal mechanisms. To solidify on these properties, we utilized two established datasets, HaluEval and TruthfulQA and convert their existing format of question answering into various other formats to narrow down these properties as the reason for the hallucinations. Our findings reveal that hallucination percentages across symbolic properties are notably high for Gemma-2-2B, averaging 79.0% across tasks and datasets. With increased model scale, hallucination drops to 73.6% for Gemma-2-9B and 63.9% for Gemma-2-27B, reflecting a 15 percentage point reduction overall. Although the hallucination rate decreases as the model size increases, a substantial amount of hallucination caused by symbolic properties still persists. This is especially evident for modifiers (ranging from 84.76% to 94.98%) and named entities (ranging from 83.87% to 93.96%) across all Gemma models and both datasets. These findings indicate that symbolic elements continue to confuse the models, pointing to a fundamental weakness in how these LLMs process such inputs--regardless of their scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09715","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09714","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09714","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09714","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serge Lionel","familyName":"Nikiema","name":"Nikiema, Serge Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albérick Euraste","familyName":"Djire","name":"Djire, Albérick Euraste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdoul Aziz","familyName":"Bonkoungou","name":"Bonkoungou, Abdoul Aziz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Micheline Bénédicte","familyName":"Moumoula","name":"Moumoula, Micheline Bénédicte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordan","familyName":"Samhi","name":"Samhi, Jordan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdoul Kader","familyName":"Kabore","name":"Kabore, Abdoul Kader","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacques","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Jacques","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tegawendé F.","familyName":"Bissyande","name":"Bissyande, Tegawendé F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Small Transformation Expose the Weakness of Semantic Similarity Measures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T11:00:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research examines how well different methods measure semantic similarity, which is important for various software engineering applications such as code search, API recommendations, automated code reviews, and refactoring tools. While large language models are increasingly used for these similarity assessments, questions remain about whether they truly understand semantic relationships or merely recognize surface patterns.\n The study tested 18 different similarity measurement approaches, including word-based methods, embedding techniques, LLM-based systems, and structure-aware algorithms. The researchers created a systematic testing framework that applies controlled changes to text and code to evaluate how well each method handles different types of semantic relationships.\n The results revealed significant issues with commonly used metrics. Some embedding-based methods incorrectly identified semantic opposites as similar up to 99.9 percent of the time, while certain transformer-based approaches occasionally rated opposite meanings as more similar than synonymous ones. The study found that embedding methods' poor performance often stemmed from how they calculate distances; switching from Euclidean distance to cosine similarity improved results by 24 to 66 percent. LLM-based approaches performed better at distinguishing semantic differences, producing low similarity scores (0.00 to 0.29) for genuinely different meanings, compared to embedding methods that incorrectly assigned high scores (0.82 to 0.99) to dissimilar content."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09714","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:54Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09713","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09713","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09713","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duolin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Duolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Jiao","name":"Jiao, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhehao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhehao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianzhen","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Lianzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjie","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HANRAG: Heuristic Accurate Noise-resistant Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Multi-hop Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T06:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) approach enhances question-answering systems and dialogue generation tasks by integrating information retrieval (IR) technologies with large language models (LLMs). This strategy, which retrieves information from external knowledge bases to bolster the response capabilities of generative models, has achieved certain successes. However, current RAG methods still face numerous challenges when dealing with multi-hop queries. For instance, some approaches overly rely on iterative retrieval, wasting too many retrieval steps on compound queries. Additionally, using the original complex query for retrieval may fail to capture content relevant to specific sub-queries, resulting in noisy retrieved content. If the noise is not managed, it can lead to the problem of noise accumulation. To address these issues, we introduce HANRAG, a novel heuristic-based framework designed to efficiently tackle problems of varying complexity. Driven by a powerful revelator, HANRAG routes queries, decomposes them into sub-queries, and filters noise from retrieved documents. This enhances the system's adaptability and noise resistance, making it highly capable of handling diverse queries. We compare the proposed framework against other leading industry methods across various benchmarks. The results demonstrate that our framework obtains superior performance in both single-hop and multi-hop question-answering tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09713","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09709","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09709","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09709","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Assisting Research Proposal Writing with Large Language Models: Evaluation and Refinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T10:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT are increasingly used in academic writing, yet issues such as incorrect or fabricated references raise ethical concerns. Moreover, current content quality evaluations often rely on subjective human judgment, which is labor-intensive and lacks objectivity, potentially compromising the consistency and reliability. In this study, to provide a quantitative evaluation and enhance research proposal writing capabilities of LLMs, we propose two key evaluation metrics--content quality and reference validity--and an iterative prompting method based on the scores derived from these two metrics. Our extensive experiments show that the proposed metrics provide an objective, quantitative framework for assessing ChatGPT's writing performance. Additionally, iterative prompting significantly enhances content quality while reducing reference inaccuracies and fabrications, addressing critical ethical challenges in academic contexts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09709","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:47Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09707","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09707","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09707","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camilo Chacón","familyName":"Sartori","name":"Sartori, Camilo Chacón","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martín Isla","familyName":"Pino","name":"Pino, Martín Isla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Pinacho-Davidson","name":"Pinacho-Davidson, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Blum","name":"Blum, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Instance-Driven Heuristic Bias In the Context of a Biased Random Key Genetic Algorithm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T21:46:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrating Large Language Models (LLMs) within metaheuristics opens a novel path for solving complex combinatorial optimization problems. While most existing approaches leverage LLMs for code generation to create or refine specific heuristics, they often overlook the structural properties of individual problem instances. In this work, we introduce a novel framework that integrates LLMs with a Biased Random-Key Genetic Algorithm (BRKGA) to solve the NP-hard Longest Run Subsequence problem. Our approach extends the instance-driven heuristic bias paradigm by introducing a human-LLM collaborative process to co-design and implement a set of computationally efficient metrics. The LLM analyzes these instance-specific metrics to generate a tailored heuristic bias, which steers the BRKGA toward promising areas of the search space. We conduct a comprehensive experimental evaluation, including rigorous statistical tests, convergence and behavioral analyses, and targeted ablation studies, comparing our method against a standard BRKGA baseline across 1,050 generated instances of varying complexity. Results show that our top-performing hybrid, BRKGA+Llama-4-Maverick, achieves statistically significant improvements over the baseline, particularly on the most complex instances. Our findings confirm that leveraging an LLM to produce an a priori, instance-driven heuristic bias is a valuable approach for enhancing metaheuristics in complex optimization domains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to a journal for review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09707","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taniya","familyName":"Gidatkar","name":"Gidatkar, Taniya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oluwaseun","familyName":"Ajao","name":"Ajao, Oluwaseun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Shardlow","name":"Shardlow, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differential Robustness in Transformer Language Models: Empirical Evaluation Under Adversarial Text Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; H.3.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T21:43:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study evaluates the resilience of large language models (LLMs) against adversarial attacks, specifically focusing on Flan-T5, BERT, and RoBERTa-Base. Using systematically designed adversarial tests through TextFooler and BERTAttack, we found significant variations in model robustness. RoBERTa-Base and FlanT5 demonstrated remarkable resilience, maintaining accuracy even when subjected to sophisticated attacks, with attack success rates of 0%. In contrast. BERT-Base showed considerable vulnerability, with TextFooler achieving a 93.75% success rate in reducing model accuracy from 48% to just 3%. Our research reveals that while certain LLMs have developed effective defensive mechanisms, these safeguards often require substantial computational resources. This study contributes to the understanding of LLM security by identifying existing strengths and weaknesses in current safeguarding approaches and proposes practical recommendations for developing more efficient and effective defensive strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 4 tables, to appear in proceedings of Recent Advances in Natural Language Processing (RANLP 2025) and ACL Anthology"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:43Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09705","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09705","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09705","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudio","familyName":"Pinhanez","name":"Pinhanez, Claudio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo","familyName":"Cavalin","name":"Cavalin, Paulo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassia","familyName":"Sanctos","name":"Sanctos, Cassia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Grave","name":"Grave, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yago","familyName":"Primerano","name":"Primerano, Yago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Non-Determinism of Small LLMs: Evidence of Low Answer Consistency in Repetition Trials of Standard Multiple-Choice Benchmarks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T17:31:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work explores the consistency of small LLMs (2B-8B parameters) in answering multiple times the same question. We present a study on known, open-source LLMs responding to 10 repetitions of questions from the multiple-choice benchmarks MMLU-Redux and MedQA, considering different inference temperatures, small vs. medium models (50B-80B), finetuned vs. base models, and other parameters. We also look into the effects of requiring multi-trial answer consistency on accuracy and the trade-offs involved in deciding which model best provides both of them. To support those studies, we propose some new analytical and graphical tools. Results show that the number of questions which can be answered consistently vary considerably among models but are typically in the 50%-80% range for small models at low inference temperatures. Also, accuracy among consistent answers seems to reasonably correlate with overall accuracy. Results for medium-sized models seem to indicate much higher levels of answer consistency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09705","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Mazyaki","name":"Mazyaki, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Naghizadeh","name":"Naghizadeh, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samaneh Ranjkhah","familyName":"Zonouzaghi","name":"Zonouzaghi, Samaneh Ranjkhah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Setareh","name":"Setareh, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Temporal Preferences in Language Models for Long-Horizon Assistance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T16:21:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study whether language models (LMs) exhibit future- versus present-oriented preferences in intertemporal choice and whether those preferences can be systematically manipulated. Using adapted human experimental protocols, we evaluate multiple LMs on time-tradeoff tasks and benchmark them against a sample of human decision makers. We introduce an operational metric, the Manipulability of Time Orientation (MTO), defined as the change in an LM's revealed time preference between future- and present-oriented prompts. In our tests, reasoning-focused models (e.g., DeepSeek-Reasoner and grok-3-mini) choose later options under future-oriented prompts but only partially personalize decisions across identities or geographies. Moreover, models that correctly reason about time orientation internalize a future orientation for themselves as AI decision makers. We discuss design implications for AI assistants that should align with heterogeneous, long-horizon goals and outline a research agenda on personalized contextual calibration and socially aware deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09703","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09703","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09703","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixiang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xubin","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Xubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengwei","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Shengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changting","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Changting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CTCC: A Robust and Stealthy Fingerprinting Framework for Large Language Models via Cross-Turn Contextual Correlation Backdoor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-05T05:59:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread deployment of large language models (LLMs) has intensified concerns around intellectual property (IP) protection, as model theft and unauthorized redistribution become increasingly feasible. To address this, model fingerprinting aims to embed verifiable ownership traces into LLMs. However, existing methods face inherent trade-offs between stealthness, robustness, and generalizability, being either detectable via distributional shifts, vulnerable to adversarial modifications, or easily invalidated once the fingerprint is revealed. In this work, we introduce CTCC, a novel rule-driven fingerprinting framework that encodes contextual correlations across multiple dialogue turns, such as counterfactual, rather than relying on token-level or single-turn triggers. CTCC enables fingerprint verification under black-box access while mitigating false positives and fingerprint leakage, supporting continuous construction under a shared semantic rule even if partial triggers are exposed. Extensive experiments across multiple LLM architectures demonstrate that CTCC consistently achieves stronger stealth and robustness than prior work. Our findings position CTCC as a reliable and practical solution for ownership verification in real-world LLM deployment scenarios. Our code and data are publicly available at ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EMNLP2025 MainConference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09703","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:39Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09690","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09690","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09690","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ping","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqiang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jianqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianqi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Qianqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunnan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chunnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Kao","name":"Kao, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajat","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Rajat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baofen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Baofen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caleb","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Caleb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangjie","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Liangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Powering Job Search at Scale: LLM-Enhanced Query Understanding in Job Matching Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-19T21:35:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3760913","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Query understanding is essential in modern relevance systems, where user queries are often short, ambiguous, and highly context-dependent. Traditional approaches often rely on multiple task-specific Named Entity Recognition models to extract structured facets as seen in job search applications. However, this fragmented architecture is brittle, expensive to maintain, and slow to adapt to evolving taxonomies and language patterns. In this paper, we introduce a unified query understanding framework powered by a Large Language Model (LLM), designed to address these limitations. Our approach jointly models the user query and contextual signals such as profile attributes to generate structured interpretations that drive more accurate and personalized recommendations. The framework improves relevance quality in online A/B testing while significantly reducing system complexity and operational overhead. The results demonstrate that our solution provides a scalable and adaptable foundation for query understanding in dynamic web applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CIKM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09690","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:21Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09689","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09689","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09689","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Thakur","name":"Thakur, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eshani","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Eshani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smruthi","familyName":"Mukund","name":"Mukund, Smruthi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Personas within Parameters: Fine-Tuning Small Language Models with Low-Rank Adapters to Mimic User Behaviors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T22:14:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A long-standing challenge in developing accurate recommendation models is simulating user behavior, mainly due to the complex and stochastic nature of user interactions. Towards this, one promising line of work has been the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) for simulating user behavior. However, aligning these general-purpose large pre-trained models with user preferences necessitates: (i) effectively and continously parsing large-scale tabular user-item interaction data, (ii) overcoming pre-training-induced inductive biases to accurately learn user specific knowledge, and (iii) achieving the former two at scale for millions of users. While most previous works have focused on complex methods to prompt an LLM or fine-tune it on tabular interaction datasets, our approach shifts the focus to extracting robust textual user representations using a frozen LLM and simulating cost-effective, resource-efficient user agents powered by fine-tuned Small Language Models (SLMs). Further, we showcase a method for training multiple low-rank adapters for groups of users or \\textit{persona}, striking an optimal balance between scalability and performance of user behavior agents. Our experiments provide compelling empirical evidence of the efficacy of our methods, demonstrating that user agents developed using our approach have the potential to bridge the gap between offline metrics and real-world performance of recommender systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09689","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09688","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.09688","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.09688","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Atif","name":"Atif, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Garonne","name":"Garonne, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Lancon","name":"Lancon, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jerome","familyName":"Lauret","name":"Lauret, Jerome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandr","familyName":"Prozorov","name":"Prozorov, Alexandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Vranovsky","name":"Vranovsky, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI-Powered Assistant for Long-Term Access to RHIC Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-18T15:16:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) at Brookhaven National Laboratory concludes 25 years of operation, preserving not only its vast data holdings ($\\sim$1 ExaByte) but also the embedded scientific knowledge becomes a critical priority. The RHIC Data and Analysis Preservation Plan (DAPP) introduces an AI-powered assistant system that provides natural language access to documentation, workflows, and software, with the aim of supporting reproducibility, education, and future discovery. Built upon Large Language Models using Retrieval-Augmented Generation and the Model Context Protocol, this assistant indexes structured and unstructured content from RHIC experiments and enables domain-adapted interaction. We report on the deployment, computational performance, ongoing multi-experiment integration, and architectural features designed for a sustainable and explainable long-term AI access. Our experience illustrates how modern AI/ML tools can transform the usability and discoverability of scientific legacy data."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09688","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-15T01:17:18Z","registered":"2025-09-15T01:17:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-15T01:17:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":83,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=subjects.subject%3A%28%22cs.CL%22+OR+%22cs.LG%22%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22long+context%22+OR+%22long-context%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22+OR+MoE+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22long+context%22+OR+%22long-context%22%29%29+AND+registered%3A%5B2025-09-14T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-15T16%3A00%3A00Z%5D\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=200\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}